window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("data-architecture", {"topics":[{"key":"data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: Debezium","Official docs: Apache Airflow"],"why":"The first version of every RAG system is a script: list everything in Confluence or an S3 bucket, embed\nit, upsert it, run it nightly. It works at 2,000 documents and fails in four predictable ways at 200,000.\n\nFirst, cost scales with the corpus rather than with change. A nightly full re-crawl re-fetches, re-parses\nand re-embeds documents that have not moved, and the embedding bill and API rate limits become the\nbottleneck long before the vector store does. Second, deletes are invisible. A listing-based crawl only\never sees what exists now; a document deleted at the source stays in the index forever and keeps being\nretrieved, which for HR policies or revoked contracts is a correctness and compliance bug. Third, a crash\nhalfway through leaves the index half new and half old with no record of which is which, and the only\nrecovery is another full run. Fourth, when the team changes the chunker or the embedding model, there is\nno raw copy to re-transform from, so \"re-index\" means \"re-crawl\", colliding again with rate limits and\nwith the source team's goodwill.\n\nThe naive fix, \"make the script faster\", does not address any of these. What is needed is a separation\nbetween *getting bytes out of sources* (which should be incremental, polite and durable) and *turning bytes\ninto retrieval units* (which should be cheap to re-run). This lesson covers the first half; the ETL/ELT\nlesson in this module covers the second.","how":"### Classify each source by how it exposes change\nEvery connector design starts with one question: how does this source tell me what changed? There are\nfour answers, in rising order of fidelity and cost.\n\n1. **Snapshot diff.** List everything, hash each item, compare with the last snapshot. Catches creates,\n   updates and deletes. Cost is O(corpus) per run for the listing (not for the content, if the source gives\n   a cheap version or ETag), so it is fine for a few hundred thousand items and wrong for a billion rows.\n2. **Cursor / watermark.** Query `WHERE updated_at > :last_seen` or use a provider's change token\n   (Microsoft Graph delta queries, Google Drive's changes feed). Cheap and incremental. Provider change\n   tokens usually do report deletions (Graph delta marks removed items, for example), but a plain\n   timestamp cursor misses hard deletes and can miss rows written with a timestamp earlier than the\n   watermark by clock skew or long transactions. The fix is to read with `>=` and a small overlap window\n   and dedupe by id, and to pair it with a periodic snapshot diff that reconciles deletes.\n3. **Change data capture (CDC).** Tail the database's transaction log (Postgres logical decoding, MySQL\n   binlog) with a tool such as Debezium and receive every insert, update and delete as an ordered event.\n   Highest fidelity, including deletes, at the cost of operating a streaming system and needing DBA\n   cooperation.\n4. **Push: webhooks and event streams.** The source calls you. Lowest latency, but webhooks are\n   at-least-once at best and often lossy, so they are a trigger to fetch, not the source of truth.\n\nThe design choice follows from freshness requirements and from what the source offers, not from taste.\nA support knowledge base that changes a few hundred times a day is fine on a 15-minute cursor poll with a\nnightly snapshot reconcile; an orders table that drives agent decisions wants CDC.\n\n### Land raw bytes immutably\nEach fetched item is written to a landing store (object storage or a lakehouse table) as the raw bytes\nplus a metadata record: `source`, `source_id`, `source_version` (ETag, revision number or content hash),\n`fetched_at`, `acl` (who may read it at the source), and the raw `uri`. The record is immutable; a new\nversion is a new row, not an overwrite. This is the single most important choice in the pattern, because\neverything downstream (parsing, chunking, embedding) is a deterministic function of the raw bytes and can\nbe replayed from them without touching the source again.\n\n### Make every stage idempotent\nThe natural key of an item is `(source, source_id)`. Content hashing on top of it lets the pipeline skip\nitems whose bytes have not changed even when the source claims an update (many SaaS APIs bump\n`updated_at` on permission changes or views). Skipping re-parsing and re-embedding is right in that case,\nbut the ACL must still be compared and propagated, because a permission change with identical bytes is\nexactly the update retrieval filtering depends on. Downstream writes are upserts keyed by that identity, and\ndeletes are written as tombstone rows carrying the same key, so a replay of any window produces the same\nend state. The watermark is committed *after* the batch's writes are durable; committing it first is the\nclassic bug that turns a crash into a silent gap.\n\nThe delivery guarantee this buys is worth stating precisely. No stage is exactly-once: the overlap window,\nretries and redelivered webhooks all mean the same change can arrive twice. The pipeline is\n*at-least-once with idempotent effects*. Re-applying a change keyed by `(source, source_id, version)`\nleaves the store unchanged, so a duplicate does no harm and a gap is the only failure you must prevent.\nThat is why the commit order matters more than deduplication: duplicates are absorbed downstream, gaps\nare not.\n\n### Initial load, backfills and the handoff to incremental\nEvery connector has two modes: the first full load and steady-state increments. The dangerous moment is\nthe switch from one to the other. If you snapshot the source and then start polling \"from now\", changes\nmade during the snapshot, which can take hours for a large source, fall between the two. The fix is to\nrecord the change position *before* the snapshot starts: the cursor value, the provider's delta token,\nor the log position for CDC. Then the incremental reader starts from that recorded position, and the\nidempotent upserts absorb the overlap. Debezium works this way: it takes a consistent initial snapshot,\nthen streams from the log offset it recorded at snapshot time. Run backfills, such as re-ingesting a\nsource after a connector bug, in a separate lane with their own rate-limit budget. Otherwise a\nmulti-day backfill uses up the API quota and the incremental sync goes stale while it runs.\n\n### Treat source schemas as a contract that will break\nSaaS APIs add fields, rename them and change pagination without notice, and a database team will\neventually drop a column the connector reads. Validate each fetched record against an expected schema\nat the landing boundary. Store unknown fields in the raw record instead of dropping them, and send\nrecords that fail validation to quarantine rather than letting them flow on with nulls. A connector\nthat quietly writes `title = null` for a whole source is worse than one that stops, because the index\nfills with untitled chunks and nothing alarms. The data-contracts lesson covers making this explicit\nwith the source owner.\n\n### Decouple fetch rate from processing rate\nSources impose rate limits; parsers and embedders have their own throughput. A queue between the connector\nand the transformation stages absorbs the mismatch, gives retries with backoff a place to live, and lets a\npoison document (a 2 GB PDF that crashes the parser) be parked in a dead-letter queue instead of stalling\nthe run. Batch orchestration (Airflow, Dagster, Prefect) suits snapshot and cursor sources; a streaming\nconsumer suits CDC and webhooks. Micro-batches every few minutes are the common middle ground.\n\n### Carry ACLs and lineage as data\nPermissions from the source travel with the document as a first-class field, because retrieval must filter\non them later and a pipeline that drops them cannot be fixed at query time. Lineage, which raw version a\nchunk came from and which pipeline version produced it, is what makes a selective re-run possible.\n\n### Observe the pipeline as a pipeline\nPer stage, count items in, items out and items failed; track lag as `now − last_committed_watermark`;\nalarm on a change ratio that is zero (connector silently broken) or near 100 % (source migrated and every\nid changed). These three numbers answer \"is the index fresh?\" without reading the index.","example":"An incremental connector loop for a cursor-based source. It reads with an overlap window, dedupes by id,\nskips re-embedding unchanged content by hash while still propagating ACL changes, writes tombstones for\ndeletes reported by the source, and commits the watermark only after the batch is durable.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, datetime as dt\n\nOVERLAP = dt.timedelta(minutes=5)          # tolerate clock skew / late commits at the source\nEPOCH = dt.datetime(1970, 1, 1, tzinfo=dt.timezone.utc)\n\ndef sync_once(source, landing, state):\n    wm = state.get(\"watermark\")                              # tz-aware datetime, or None on first run\n    since = wm - OVERLAP if wm else EPOCH\n    seen, newest = set(), wm or EPOCH\n    for item in source.list_changes(since=since):          # yields {id, updated_at, deleted, acl, fetch}\n        if item[\"id\"] in seen:\n            continue                                         # same id twice in one listing (paged API)\n        seen.add(item[\"id\"])\n        newest = max(newest, item[\"updated_at\"])\n        key = (source.name, item[\"id\"])\n        if item[\"deleted\"]:\n            landing.write_tombstone(key, at=item[\"updated_at\"])   # idempotent if re-delivered\n            continue\n        raw = item[\"fetch\"]()                                # bytes; respects source rate limits\n        digest = hashlib.sha256(raw).hexdigest()\n        if landing.latest_hash(key) == digest:               # overlap re-delivery or metadata-only bump\n            if landing.latest_acl(key) != item.get(\"acl\"):\n                landing.update_acl(key, item.get(\"acl\"))     # permissions changed: no re-embed needed\n                landing.emit_event(\"document.acl_changed\", key=key)\n            continue\n        landing.write_version(key, raw, hash=digest, acl=item.get(\"acl\"),\n                              fetched_at=dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc))\n        landing.emit_event(\"document.changed\", key=key, hash=digest)\n    landing.flush()                                          # make writes durable first ...\n    state[\"watermark\"] = newest                              # ... then advance the cursor\n    return len(seen)\n```\n\nA nightly snapshot-diff job runs alongside this loop to catch deletes the source never reported, which is\nthe standard pairing for sources that only expose `updated_at`.","tradeoffs":["Snapshot diff is the simplest way to catch deletes and needs no source cooperation, but its listing cost grows with the corpus, so it flips to cursor-plus-reconcile once a full listing takes longer than the freshness window.","CDC gives exact, ordered, delete-inclusive change streams, but it requires log access, DBA buy-in and a streaming platform; for a source changing a few hundred times a day, polling is cheaper to run.","Storing every raw version makes replays and audits trivial at the cost of storage; when documents are large binaries that change often, keep only the last N versions and rely on lineage for the rest.","Streaming ingestion cuts freshness lag to seconds but spreads work evenly, which is worse for embedding APIs that are cheaper and faster in large batches; micro-batching usually wins unless the use case is truly real-time."],"pitfalls":["Advancing the watermark before the batch's writes are durable; the symptom is documents that are missing from the index after a crash with no error in any log.","Using a timestamp cursor without an overlap window; the symptom is sporadic missed updates traced to rows committed late or from a clock-skewed replica.","Never handling deletes; the symptom is a retrieved answer citing a document that no longer exists at the source, often discovered by a customer rather than a dashboard.","Dropping source ACLs during ingestion \"to simplify\"; the symptom is a user retrieving a document they could not open in the source system, which is a security incident, not a bug.","Re-fetching on every `updated_at` bump without a content hash; the symptom is a large embedding bill for a corpus that barely changes, because the SaaS source bumps timestamps on permission edits. The opposite error, skipping hash-identical items entirely, silently drops those permission edits.","Starting the incremental cursor at the time the initial snapshot *finished* rather than when it started; the symptom is documents edited during the first load that stay at their old version until someone edits them again.","No dead-letter path; the symptom is a whole nightly run that fails repeatedly on the same oversized or malformed file while thousands of healthy documents go stale."],"when":"Reach for this pattern the moment a corpus has more than one source, changes while the system is live, or\nmust respect deletions and permissions, which is to say for anything beyond a demo. Measure freshness lag,\nchange ratio per run, per-stage failure counts and the embedding spend per run; those four numbers expose\nnearly every ingestion defect. Do not build CDC for a wiki that changes twice a day, and do not accept a\n\"full re-crawl nightly\" design for a corpus whose embedding cost per full pass exceeds the monthly vector\nstore bill. In day-to-day work this is the code that pages you at 3 a.m. when a source's API changes; in\ninterviews it is the \"design ingestion for SharePoint plus Jira plus a Postgres orders table, with\ndeletes and permissions\" question, and the expected answer walks through change detection per source,\nimmutable raw landing, idempotent upserts and tombstones, and the watermark commit order.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes connectors with cursors, overlap windows, content hashing and tombstones, and instruments lag and change ratio so a silent connector failure is visible within one polling interval.","architect":"Decides per source between snapshot diff, cursor polling, CDC and push, defines the landing-store schema including ACL and lineage fields, and sets the freshness SLA that justifies the cost.","fde":"Negotiates API access, rate limits and service accounts with the customer's source-system owners, and proves deletions and permission changes propagate before go-live, because that is the question their security team will ask."},"check":{"q":"A customer's RAG index is built by a nightly job that lists every page in their wiki and embeds\npages whose `updated_at` changed. Users report that deleted pages still appear in answers and that some\nedits never show up. Diagnose both symptoms and describe the minimal redesign.","a":"Deleted pages persist because a listing of current pages can only report what exists; nothing\never emits a delete for a page that is gone, so the index keeps its chunks. Missed edits come from the\ntimestamp cursor: a page saved just before the previous run's watermark but committed after the listing\nwas taken, or saved on a replica with skewed time, falls below the cursor and is never re-read. The\nminimal fix keeps the nightly job but makes it a snapshot diff: hash the current page list and content\nversions, compare with the stored snapshot, and emit tombstones for ids that disappeared and updates for\nchanged hashes. Add an overlap window to the cursor read and dedupe by id, commit the watermark only\nafter writes are durable, and store the raw page bytes so chunker changes can be replayed without\nre-crawling the wiki."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should the ingestion watermark be committed only after the batch's writes are durable?","options":["Because the watermark must be the largest timestamp in the batch, which is unknown until the end","Because a crash between committing the watermark and persisting the writes would silently skip those items on the next run","Because committing earlier would cause duplicate writes in the landing store","Because the source system rejects queries with a watermark newer than its own clock"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If the cursor advances first and the process dies before the writes land, the next run starts after the lost items and nothing ever re-reads them. Duplicates are the safe failure; gaps are the silent one."},{"q":"A source exposes only an `updated_at` field and a list-all endpoint. Which design catches deletes?","options":["Polling `updated_at` with an overlap window and deduplicating by id","Subscribing to the source's webhooks and treating each event as authoritative","Running a periodic snapshot diff of the listed ids against the previously stored snapshot","Hashing document content and skipping items whose hash has not changed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A timestamp cursor never sees a row that no longer exists, and webhooks are lossy. Only comparing the set of ids present now with the set stored last time reveals which ids vanished, so tombstones can be emitted for them."},{"q":"What is the main reason to store immutable raw bytes in a landing store rather than only the derived chunks?","options":["Raw bytes compress better than chunk text in object storage","Vector databases cannot store the original document","Parsing, chunking and embedding are re-run far more often than sources change, and replaying from raw avoids re-crawling","Regulators require a copy of every source document"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The transformation steps are where experimentation and model upgrades happen. With a raw copy, a new chunker is a local re-run; without it, every change becomes a full re-crawl against rate-limited sources."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/etl-elt-for-rag","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: dbt","Anthropic, 2024 — Introducing Contextual Retrieval"],"why":"The terms come from data warehousing. ETL was the norm when warehouse storage and compute were expensive:\nyou cleaned and shaped data on a separate server and loaded only the final tables. ELT took over when\ncloud warehouses and lakes made storage cheap and put elastic compute next to the data: load everything\nraw, then transform with SQL (the dbt workflow) and keep every intermediate table. The reason ELT won is\nthat transformations are where requirements change, and keeping raw data means a changed transformation\nis a re-run, not a re-extraction.\n\nRAG pipelines rediscover this the hard way. The first implementation is one function: fetch a document,\nsplit it into 512-token chunks, embed, upsert. Then retrieval quality disappoints and the team wants to\ntry semantic chunking, add section titles to each chunk, switch embedding models, or prepend an\nLLM-generated summary to each chunk (contextual retrieval). Each of these means re-running everything\nfrom the source, because nothing in between was kept. There is no way to A/B two chunkers against the\nsame parsed text, no intermediate artifact to inspect when a retrieval failure is traced to a garbled\nchunk, and no cache of embeddings so the second attempt costs as much as the first. Worse, a vector\ndatabase is a poor transformation engine: it cannot join, deduplicate or re-chunk, so transforming\n\"inside the target\" in the warehouse sense is not even an option. The resolution is to treat the\nchunk-and-embedding tables as the real data product and the index as a projection of them.","how":"### The stage tables\nModel the pipeline as a chain of derived tables, each keyed so that it can be recomputed incrementally:\n\n1. `documents`: raw bytes (or a pointer) plus `doc_id`, `doc_version` (content hash) and source metadata\n   including ACLs. Produced by ingestion; immutable per version.\n2. `parsed`: text plus structure (headings, tables, page numbers) per `(doc_id, doc_version, parser_version)`.\n   Parsing is the most expensive and most bug-prone step, so its output is stored, not recomputed.\n3. `chunks`: `chunk_id`, `doc_id`, `doc_version`, `chunker_version`, `position`, `text`, `section_path`,\n   `metadata`. Make `chunk_id = hash(doc_id, doc_version, chunker_version, position)` so a new chunker\n   produces new ids and old chunks can be found and deleted as orphans rather than overwritten in place.\n4. `enriched` (optional): the chunk text with additions that improve retrieval, such as the document\n   title and section path prepended, extracted entities, or a short LLM-written context sentence placed\n   before the chunk (contextual retrieval). Keep this separate from `chunks` because enrichment calls an\n   LLM and is the step most likely to be re-tuned.\n5. `embeddings`: `(chunk_id, embedding_model, vector)`. Cache by `(embedding_model, text_hash)` so an\n   unchanged chunk is never embedded twice, and so two embedding models can coexist during a migration.\n6. The index: an upsert of `embeddings` joined to `chunks` metadata, keyed by `chunk_id`, followed by a\n   delete of ids present in the index but absent from the current `chunks` for that document.\n\nEach table is a deterministic function of the previous one plus a version parameter. That is what makes\nthe pipeline incremental: when `chunker_version` changes, only `chunks`, `enriched`, `embeddings` and the\nindex are recomputed, and only for documents (all of them, in this case, but from `parsed`, not from the\nsource); when one document changes, only its rows flow through every stage.\n\n### Why materialize instead of streaming through\nStoring intermediates costs disk and adds a write per stage. It buys four things. Debuggability: a bad\nanswer traces to a chunk, the chunk to its parsed text, the parsed text to the raw bytes, each inspectable.\nExperimentation: two chunkers or two embedding models run against the same `parsed` table and are\ncompared with the same retrieval eval set. Cost control: embedding and LLM enrichment, the expensive\ncalls, are cached by content hash and never repeated for unchanged text. Late binding: the embedding\nmodel is a parameter of the last stage, so upgrading it is a re-run of one stage, not of the pipeline.\n\n### Where ETL still applies\nSome transformations must happen before anything is stored, which is ETL in the strict sense: redacting\nPII that may not be persisted, dropping documents whose classification forbids them in this system, and\nenforcing tenant boundaries at the landing store. The rule is that compliance-driven transformations\nbelong before the load and quality-driven transformations belong after it.\n\n### Quality gates between stages\nBecause every stage always produces *something*, failures are silent unless measured. Standard gates:\ncharacters per page from the parser (a scanned PDF parsed as text yields near zero), empty or\nnear-duplicate chunk ratio, embedding failure rate, and orphan count after index reconciliation. A gate\nthat trips stops promotion of that stage's output, which is cheaper than finding out from users.\n\n### Orchestration and incrementality\nA DAG scheduler (Dagster, Airflow, Prefect, or dbt-style SQL models over a lakehouse) runs stages in\ndependency order on partitions of changed documents. Writes to the index are idempotent upserts by\n`chunk_id`, so a retried stage is harmless; the version columns are what make \"re-run everything from\n`parsed` with chunker v3\" a one-line change rather than a project.\n\nThe rule for deciding what to recompute is the same one a build system uses. Each row's key includes\nthe hash of its inputs plus the version of the function that produced it, and a stage runs only for\nkeys whose output row does not exist yet. A document edit changes `doc_version`, so new keys appear in\nevery stage for that document only. A chunker change changes `chunker_version`, so new keys appear\nfrom `chunks` onward for every document. An embedding-model change appears only in `embeddings`.\nNothing has to track \"what changed\"; the missing keys are the work list.\n\n### Not every change needs a re-embed\nSome changes only touch metadata: an ACL edit, a document moved to a new folder, a retention label.\nThese should go to the index as a payload update keyed by `doc_id`, without re-chunking or re-embedding,\nbecause the vectors did not change. The pipeline needs two paths, a content path through every stage\nand a metadata path straight to the index payload. Without the metadata path, a permission change\neither triggers an expensive full re-run or never reaches the index, and the second outcome is a\nsecurity bug.","example":"A content-addressed transform stage: chunk ids include the chunker version, embeddings are cached by\n`(model, text_hash)`, and index reconciliation deletes orphaned chunks of re-chunked documents. The\nstores are stand-ins for lakehouse tables and a vector database.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json\n\nCHUNKER_VERSION, EMBED_MODEL = \"v3-semantic\", \"text-embedding-3-small\"\n\ndef h(*parts):\n    return hashlib.sha256(\"|\".join(map(str, parts)).encode()).hexdigest()[:24]\n\ndef build_chunks(doc_id, doc_version, parsed_sections, chunker):\n    rows = []\n    for pos, (section_path, text) in enumerate(chunker(parsed_sections)):\n        rows.append({\"chunk_id\": h(doc_id, doc_version, CHUNKER_VERSION, pos),\n                     \"doc_id\": doc_id, \"doc_version\": doc_version,\n                     \"chunker_version\": CHUNKER_VERSION, \"position\": pos,\n                     \"text\": f\"{section_path}\\n{text}\",            # cheap enrichment: section path\n                     \"text_hash\": h(section_path, text)})\n    return rows\n\ndef embed_stage(chunks, cache, embed_fn):\n    todo = [c for c in chunks if (EMBED_MODEL, c[\"text_hash\"]) not in cache]\n    for c, vec in zip(todo, embed_fn([c[\"text\"] for c in todo])):   # batched, rate-limited call\n        cache[(EMBED_MODEL, c[\"text_hash\"])] = vec\n    return [(c[\"chunk_id\"], cache[(EMBED_MODEL, c[\"text_hash\"])], c) for c in chunks]\n\ndef reconcile_index(index, doc_id, rows):\n    live = {cid for cid, _, _ in rows}\n    for cid, vec, meta in rows:\n        index.upsert(cid, vec, {k: meta[k] for k in (\"doc_id\", \"doc_version\", \"position\")})\n    stale = index.ids_for_doc(doc_id) - live                       # chunks from an older chunker version\n    index.delete(stale)\n    return {\"upserted\": len(live), \"deleted\": len(stale)}\n```\n\nSwitching chunkers is now `CHUNKER_VERSION = \"v4\"` plus a re-run from the parsed table. Unchanged text\nstill hits the embedding cache, and `reconcile_index` removes the v3 chunks of each document as its v4\nchunks land. Three details in the code are deliberate. `reconcile_index` upserts before it deletes. A\nreader can briefly see old and new chunks of a document together, which is better than briefly seeing\nnone of them. A source deletion needs no special path: calling `reconcile_index(index, doc_id, [])`\ndeletes every chunk of that document. And `text_hash` deliberately leaves out `doc_version`. When one\nparagraph of a 40-chunk document is edited, all 40 chunk ids change because the version changed, but\n39 texts hash the same, so only one embedding call is made. This holds for structure-aligned chunking,\nwhere boundaries follow sections and paragraphs. With fixed-size token windows, an edit that changes the\nlength of the text moves every later boundary, so every later chunk gets a new hash. Chunk-boundary\nstability is therefore a cost property as well as a quality one.\n\nThe cache matters most for the expensive optional stage. Take 2 million documents at 4,000 tokens and\n12 chunks each, which is 24 million chunks. Embedding them reads each chunk once: about 24 M × 400 ≈\n10 billion tokens per full pass. Contextual enrichment is far bigger. Each chunk's LLM call usually\nincludes the whole document as context, so it reads about 24 M × 4,400 ≈ 100 billion input tokens,\nabout ten times the embedding pass, plus an output sentence per chunk. Prompt caching of the shared\ndocument prefix reduces that, and a content-hash cache means a chunker change does not repeat it for\ntext that did not move. Run this arithmetic before choosing which stages to re-run and how often.","tradeoffs":["Materializing every stage costs storage and one extra write per stage, but it flips to a clear win as soon as the team changes a chunker or embedding model for the second time.","ELT keeps raw data available for any future transformation, but compliance rules that forbid storing certain content force those specific transformations back before the load.","Content-addressed chunk ids make orphans detectable and re-runs idempotent, yet any change to the chunker version re-embeds the whole corpus; a cache keyed by text hash limits that to chunks whose text actually changed.","LLM-based enrichment (summaries, contextual prefixes) can raise retrieval quality noticeably, but it adds an LLM call per chunk per version, which dominates pipeline cost at corpus scale unless cached and applied selectively."],"pitfalls":["Deriving chunk ids from `(doc_id, position)` only and skipping orphan reconciliation; the symptom after a chunker change is old and new chunks of the same document both ranking in the top-k, often with overlapping text, because a new chunker that emits fewer chunks overwrites positions 0..n−1 and leaves the old tail live. Even with reconciliation, position-only ids overwrite in place, so a half-finished re-run leaves an index of mixed chunker versions with no way to tell which rows are which.","Mixing vectors from two embedding models in one index; the symptom is retrieval quality dropping right after a model \"upgrade\", because similarity scores from different models are not comparable.","Embedding before near-duplicate removal; the symptom is the top-k filled with copies of the same boilerplate paragraph and the useful passage pushed below the cutoff.","Transforming inside the vector database's ingest hooks; the symptom is a re-index that takes days and a pipeline that cannot be moved to another vendor without a rewrite.","No quality gate after parsing; the symptom is a retrieval failure traced, weeks later, to thousands of chunks containing nothing but page headers or OCR noise.","Treating the index as the system of record; the symptom is an outage or a vendor migration that requires re-crawling every source because the chunk tables never existed."],"when":"Use staged ELT for any RAG corpus you expect to re-chunk, re-embed or audit, which is every corpus that\nlives longer than a prototype. The signal that you need it is a question like \"what would it cost to try\na different chunk size?\" being answered in weeks rather than hours. Measure embedding cache hit rate,\nre-run time per stage, orphan count after reconciliation and parse yield; these show whether the pipeline\nis incremental in practice. Skip the full apparatus for a static corpus of a few thousand files that will\nbe rebuilt from scratch when anything changes, but still keep the raw copy. In interviews, the expected\nanswer to \"how would you upgrade the embedding model on a live system\" is a re-run of the embedding stage\ninto a second index version from stored chunks, followed by an evaluated cutover, and that answer is only\npossible if the chunk table exists.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements stage functions with version parameters and content hashes, caches embedding calls by text hash, and writes the reconciliation step that deletes orphans after re-chunking.","architect":"Defines the chunk and embedding tables as the data product, sets which transformations must run before storage for compliance, and chooses the orchestrator and lakehouse that host the stages.","fde":"Shows the customer the parsed and chunked intermediates when a retrieval answer is wrong, so the fix is traced to a stage rather than blamed on \"the model\", and estimates re-index cost from them."},"check":{"q":"Your team wants to move from fixed 512-token chunks to a semantic chunker and, a month later, to a\nnew embedding model, on a corpus of two million documents that is live for customers. What does the\npipeline need so each change is a bounded, reversible job rather than a quarter-long project?","a":"It needs stored, versioned intermediates. The parsed table lets the semantic chunker run without\nre-crawling or re-parsing two million documents. Chunk ids that include the chunker version let the new\nchunks land beside the old ones and let reconciliation delete the old per document, so the cutover is\ngradual and reversible. An embedding cache keyed by model and text hash means only genuinely new chunk\ntext is embedded. For the model change, the embedding stage is re-run with a new model parameter into a\nsecond index version, evaluated against the same retrieval test set, and traffic is switched when it wins;\nthe old index remains for rollback. Quality gates on parse yield and orphan count catch regressions before\nusers do, and the vector index is treated as a projection that can always be rebuilt from the tables."},"quiz":[{"q":"In a RAG pipeline, why is the vector index treated as a derived structure rather than the system of record?","options":["Vector databases cannot store document metadata reliably","Chunking, enrichment and embedding change often, and keeping the chunk tables lets any of them be re-run without re-extracting from sources","Vector indexes are always rebuilt nightly regardless of changes","Regulations require a relational copy of every embedded chunk"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The transformations are the volatile part of a RAG system. If the index is the only copy, every chunker or model change becomes a re-crawl; if chunk and embedding tables exist, the index is a cheap projection that can be rebuilt, versioned or moved to another vendor."},{"q":"Which transformation genuinely belongs before the load (ETL) rather than after it (ELT) in a RAG pipeline?","options":["Splitting documents into chunks","Prepending section titles to chunk text","Redacting personal data that policy forbids persisting anywhere in the system","Computing embeddings with the current model"],"answer":2,"explanation":"ELT is preferable for quality-driven steps because they are re-tuned often. Compliance-driven steps are different: if the data may not be stored, it must be removed before the landing write, since a raw copy would itself be a violation."},{"q":"After switching chunkers, users see overlapping passages from the same document twice in the top-k. What is the likely cause?","options":["The embedding model was changed at the same time","The reranker was not retrained on the new chunk sizes","Old chunks were never reconciled away: ids lacked the chunker version and no step deleted ids absent from the new chunk set","The new chunker produces chunks that are too small for the index"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When ids depend only on document and position, a new chunker overwrites some old rows and leaves others (the tail beyond its own chunk count) live. Versioned ids plus a reconciliation step that deletes every indexed id for the document not in the current chunk set make the old chunks discoverable and removable."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/training-data-pipelines","sources":["Lee et al., 2021 — Deduplicating Training Data Makes Language Models Better","Penedo et al., 2024 — The FineWeb Datasets: Decanting the Web for the Finest Text Data at Scale","Raffel et al., 2020 — Exploring the Limits of Transfer Learning with a Unified Text-to-Text Transformer","Brown et al., 2020 — Language Models are Few-Shot Learners"],"why":"The naive pipeline is \"export a JSONL file and point the trainer at it\". It fails in ways that are\ninvisible in the loss curve and expensive to discover afterwards.\n\nDuplicates are the first problem. Web and enterprise corpora contain the same page, template or ticket\nthousands of times. A model trained on them sees those sequences many more times than the rest of the\ndata, memorizes them, and spends capacity on boilerplate; Lee et al. found that near-duplicate removal\nmade models emit memorized training text roughly ten times less often and reach the same or better\naccuracy in fewer training steps.\nContamination is the second: if the eval set's questions leaked into training through a scraped forum or\nan internal doc, the benchmark score is a measure of memorization, and the regression you see at\ndeployment has no explanation in your dashboards. Third, formatting noise: SFT examples assembled by hand\nwith inconsistent chat templates, stray system prompts or missing end-of-turn tokens teach the model the\nnoise, and the symptom is a fine-tuned model that stops at the wrong place or emits template fragments.\nFourth, reproducibility: without a dataset version and a deterministic shuffle, a loss divergence cannot\nbe reproduced, a resumed run does not match the original, and a regression cannot be attributed to a data\nchange. Fifth, throughput: tokenizing, packing and globally shuffling raw text inside the dataloader is\nCPU- and I/O-bound work that can leave the accelerators idle in a sawtooth pattern, most visibly for\nsmaller models whose per-GPU token consumption is high.\n\nThe pipeline exists to make these properties structural rather than hoped for: filter, deduplicate,\ndecontaminate, format, tokenize, pack and shard offline, and record what came from where.","how":"### Stage order, and why\nThe stages run in an order chosen so that cheap operations shrink the data before expensive ones run.\n\n1. **Sourcing and provenance.** Every source gets a manifest: origin, license or contractual basis,\n   snapshot date, raw hash. This is the only point where that information is cheap to capture.\n2. **Heuristic filtering.** Language identification, length bounds, symbol-to-word ratio, fraction of\n   lines ending in terminal punctuation, repeated-line ratio, blocklisted domains. These are the C4 and\n   Gopher-style rules: cheap per document, remove a large fraction of junk, and run first because\n   everything after is costlier per byte.\n3. **Deduplication.** Exact dedup by document hash catches byte-identical copies only. Near-duplicate\n   detection uses MinHash: shingle each document into overlapping n-grams, hash each shingle with *k*\n   hash functions and keep the minimum per function to form a signature, then band the signature with\n   locality-sensitive hashing so documents sharing a band become candidate pairs, and cluster candidates\n   with union-find, keeping one per cluster. It exists because exact hashing misses the same article with\n   a different footer, and pairwise comparison of a billion documents is impossible.\n   The reason it works: for one random hash function, the chance that two documents have the same\n   minimum shingle hash equals their Jaccard similarity *s*. So a signature of *k* minima estimates\n   *s*. Split the signature into *b* bands of *r* rows (with *k = b·r*). Two documents become a candidate\n   pair if all *r* values in at least one band match, which happens with probability\n   `P(s) = 1 − (1 − s^r)^b`. That is an S-curve with its steep part near `(1/b)^(1/r)`. With *k* = 100\n   split as *b* = 20, *r* = 5, the threshold is about 0.55. Pairs with *s* = 0.8 become candidates with\n   probability about 0.9996, pairs at 0.5 about 0.47, and pairs at 0.3 about 0.05. More rows per band\n   moves the threshold up and makes the curve steeper. Candidates are usually checked against a real\n   similarity before they are merged, because LSH controls recall, not precision.\n4. **Decontamination.** Compare training text against every evaluation set the model will be judged on,\n   using n-gram overlap (the GPT-3 report used 13-gram overlap; the right *n* is a judgement between\n   missing paraphrases and deleting common phrases), and remove or flag overlapping training documents.\n   This must be redone whenever an eval set is added. Your own validation split needs the same care.\n   Split *after* near-duplicate clustering and by group (customer, thread, product), so a cluster never\n   has members on both sides. A random row-level split of support tickets puts near-identical tickets in\n   train and validation, and the validation loss then reports memorization.\n5. **Model-based quality filtering.** A small classifier or a perplexity threshold from a reference model\n   ranks remaining documents for \"educational\" or domain quality. It runs late because it is the most\n   expensive per document and operates on the smallest set. The FineWeb work is a good public reference\n   for how much each of these stages removes on web data.\n6. **Safety and PII scrubbing.** Regex and NER passes for emails, keys, phone numbers, plus toxicity\n   filters, with the policy decided by legal rather than by the pipeline author.\n7. **Formatting.** For pretraining, documents are concatenated with an end-of-sequence token between\n   them. For SFT, each example is rendered through the exact chat template the model will serve with,\n   and the loss mask is computed so that only assistant tokens contribute to the loss; the prompt tokens\n   get the ignore index. Doing this offline, deterministically, removes a whole class of template bugs.\n8. **Tokenization and packing.** Tokenize offline and store token ids. Pack multiple short examples into\n   fixed-length sequences to avoid padding waste (a batch of 4,096-token slots filled with 300-token\n   examples is over 90 % padding otherwise). Packing requires either resetting position ids and masking\n   attention across document boundaries or accepting a small amount of cross-document attention; which\n   you choose depends on the trainer's support.\n9. **Sharding and mixture.** Write fixed-size shards (Parquet, Arrow, WebDataset tar, or a streaming\n   format such as MDS) with a shard index, so the loader can shuffle at shard level and within a buffer,\n   resume from a global sample offset, and sample sources according to a mixture weight (for example\n   up-weighting the customer's domain data relative to general text). A mixture weight implies a\n   repetition count: `epochs_of_source = weight × total_training_tokens / source_tokens`. Giving a\n   50M-token domain source 20 % of a 1B-token run means 200M tokens drawn from it, which is four passes\n   over it. Write that number down per source, because a small source with a high weight is repeated\n   many times, and repetition is where memorization and overfitting come from.\n10. **Versioning.** A dataset version is the ordered list of shard hashes plus the pipeline config. The\n    training run records that version; lineage goes from any training sample back to its source manifest.\n\n### The dataloader contract\nThe loader's job at training time is reduced to reading shards, buffered shuffling with a seeded RNG, and\ncollation. Its throughput must exceed the model's consumption in tokens per second with headroom, which\nis why the expensive work was moved offline. Prefetching and multiple workers handle the rest.","example":"Offline SFT formatting and packing: render the chat template, mask prompt tokens with the ignore index,\npack examples into fixed-length sequences separated by EOS, and emit shards with a content hash for the\ndataset manifest. The tokenizer is any Hugging Face tokenizer with a chat template.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json\nIGNORE, SEQ_LEN, SHARD_ROWS = -100, 4096, 2048\n\ndef render(tok, messages):\n    prompt = tok.apply_chat_template(messages[:-1], tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=True)\n    full   = tok.apply_chat_template(messages, tokenize=False)\n    enc = lambda s: tok(s, add_special_tokens=False)[\"input_ids\"]   # template already has BOS\n    p_ids, f_ids = enc(prompt), enc(full)\n    assert f_ids[:len(p_ids)] == p_ids, \"template must be prefix-stable\"\n    labels = [IGNORE] * len(p_ids) + f_ids[len(p_ids):]      # loss only on the final assistant turn\n    return f_ids, labels\n\ndef pack(examples, tok):\n    ids, labels, doc_ids = [], [], []\n    for n, msgs in enumerate(examples):\n        i, l = render(tok, msgs)\n        if len(i) + 1 > SEQ_LEN:\n            continue                                            # too long: drop (or truncate) and count it\n        if len(ids) + len(i) + 1 > SEQ_LEN:\n            yield ids, labels, doc_ids; ids, labels, doc_ids = [], [], []\n        ids += i + [tok.eos_token_id]; labels += l + [tok.eos_token_id]; doc_ids += [n] * (len(i) + 1)\n    if ids:\n        yield ids, labels, doc_ids                              # trainer pads this last one\n\ndef write_shards(packed, path):\n    manifest, rows = [], []\n    def flush():\n        blob = json.dumps(rows).encode(); name = f\"{path}/shard-{len(manifest):05d}.json\"\n        with open(name, \"wb\") as f: f.write(blob)\n        manifest.append({\"shard\": name, \"rows\": len(rows), \"sha256\": hashlib.sha256(blob).hexdigest()})\n    for ids, labels, doc_ids in packed:\n        rows.append({\"input_ids\": ids, \"labels\": labels, \"doc_ids\": doc_ids})  # doc_ids -> attention mask\n        if len(rows) == SHARD_ROWS:\n            flush(); rows = []\n    if rows:\n        flush()                                                 # keep the final partial shard\n    with open(f\"{path}/manifest.json\", \"w\") as f:\n        json.dump(manifest, f)                                  # the dataset version is this file's hash\n```\n\nThe `doc_ids` column lets a trainer that supports it block attention across packed documents; the manifest\nof shard hashes is what the training config references so the run is reproducible.","tradeoffs":["Aggressive heuristic filtering removes junk cheaply but also removes legitimate code, tables and non-English text; the thresholds flip when the target domain is exactly the content the rules distrust.","Near-duplicate removal improves generalization and reduces memorization, but a low similarity threshold deletes templated-but-distinct documents such as product pages, which can matter for a domain model.","Packing eliminates padding waste and raises tokens per second, but without cross-document attention masking it lets examples attend to unrelated neighbours; for short SFT examples the masking is worth it.","Offline tokenization maximizes training throughput and reproducibility, yet it binds the dataset to one tokenizer, so a tokenizer or model-family change means re-tokenizing from the text tables.","Strict n-gram decontamination protects eval validity but at small *n* removes documents that merely share common phrases with the eval set, shrinking the training set for no gain."],"pitfalls":["Decontaminating with exact match only; the symptom is a benchmark score that collapses on a freshly written eval set of the same difficulty.","Rendering SFT examples with a template that differs from serving; the symptom is the fine-tuned model emitting role tags or failing to stop at the end of its turn.","Tokenizing, packing or globally shuffling raw text inside the dataloader at scale; the symptom is GPU utilization oscillating between near zero and full while CPUs are saturated.","Non-deterministic shuffling without a seeded, offset-addressable order; the symptom is a resumed run whose loss curve does not continue the original, and a divergence nobody can reproduce.","Running near-dup detection before cheap filters; the symptom is MinHash jobs that take days on data most of which is then discarded by a one-line length rule.","Splitting train and validation at random rows instead of by group after dedup; the symptom is a validation loss that keeps falling while quality on genuinely new customers or threads stays flat.","Dropping provenance at the first join; the symptom is a takedown or license question that can only be answered by retraining without the disputed source."],"when":"Build this pipeline, at whatever scale, the moment training data is assembled from more than one source\nor will be used more than once. For a fine-tune on a few thousand customer examples the stages are small\nscripts, but the properties still matter: template-exact rendering, loss masking, dedup against the eval\nset, and a manifest hash in the training config. Measure the fraction removed at each stage, the\nnear-duplicate cluster size distribution, overlap rate with each eval set, padding ratio after packing,\nand loader tokens per second against model consumption. In interviews, \"how would you prepare data to\nfine-tune on a customer's support tickets\" is answered by walking these stages, and the differentiating\npoints are decontamination against the eval set, prompt-token loss masking and a reproducible dataset\nversion.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the filtering, MinHash dedup, template rendering and packing jobs, verifies the loss mask on a handful of examples by eye, and checks loader throughput before launching a run.","architect":"Sets the stage order and the quality, PII and licensing policies, chooses the shard format and storage layout, and requires that every run references a hashed dataset version.","fde":"Assembles customer data into the pipeline with provenance intact, agrees PII rules with the customer's legal team, and holds back the eval set from the data the customer hands over for training."},"check":{"q":"A fine-tuned support model scores well on the team's internal benchmark but performs no better than\nthe base model on live tickets, and it sometimes ends its replies with a stray assistant tag. Which\npipeline stages would you inspect first and why?","a":"Two stages explain both symptoms. The benchmark gap points to contamination: the internal eval set\nwas probably drawn from the same ticket export as the training data, so near-identical tickets appear in\nboth and the benchmark measures memorization; check n-gram overlap between training shards and the eval\nset and decontaminate or rebuild the eval from tickets dated after the export. The stray tag points to\nformatting: examples were rendered with a chat template that differs from the serving template, or the\nloss mask included prompt tokens so the model learned to reproduce template structure; inspect a few\nrendered examples token by token, confirm the ignore index covers the prompt and that the end-of-turn\ntoken terminates the labels. Near-duplicate inflation in the training set would compound both, so look at\nthe dedup cluster sizes as well."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do near-duplicate removal with MinHash rather than relying on exact document hashing?","options":["MinHash is faster than computing a SHA-256 per document","Most duplicates differ in small ways such as footers or timestamps, which exact hashing misses, and MinHash with LSH finds similar pairs without comparing every pair","Exact hashing cannot be parallelized across shards","MinHash also detects contamination against evaluation sets"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Real corpora contain the same content with trivial variations. MinHash signatures approximate shingle similarity, and LSH banding turns the impossible all-pairs comparison into candidate lookups, so near duplicates are found at scale; decontamination is a separate n-gram check."},{"q":"In SFT formatting, what does setting prompt tokens' labels to the ignore index achieve?","options":["It removes the prompt from the model's context so it only sees the answer","It prevents the tokenizer from splitting special template tokens","It excludes prompt tokens from the loss so the model learns to produce assistant turns rather than to reproduce prompts and templates","It allows examples to be packed without attention masking"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The prompt stays in the input, so the model conditions on it, but gradients only flow from assistant tokens. Training on prompt tokens wastes capacity and teaches the model to emit template structure, which shows up as stray role tags at inference."},{"q":"What is the strongest reason to tokenize and pack offline rather than in the training dataloader?","options":["Offline tokenization produces smaller files than raw text","The trainer cannot apply chat templates at runtime","Tokenization and packing are CPU-bound; done at training time they bottleneck the loader and leave accelerators idle, and done offline they are also reproducible","Packing is only possible when all examples are known in advance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When accelerators consume tokens faster than the loader's CPU workers can tokenize and pack them, the result is a sawtooth utilization pattern; whether that happens depends on model size and worker count, but the reproducibility benefit holds regardless. Precomputed shards make the loader a simple reader and make the exact sequence of training tokens part of the versioned dataset."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/lakehouse","sources":["Armbrust et al., 2021 — Lakehouse: A New Generation of Open Platforms that Unify Data Warehousing and Advanced Analytics","Armbrust et al., 2020 — Delta Lake: High-Performance ACID Table Storage over Cloud Object Stores","Official docs: Apache Iceberg","Official docs: Apache Parquet"],"why":"Before lakehouses, teams ran two tiers. A data lake (files in S3 or similar) held raw and semi-structured\ndata cheaply and let Spark or Python read it, but a directory of Parquet files is not a table: a writer\nthat dies halfway leaves readers seeing a partial dataset, two concurrent writers corrupt each other, there\nis no schema to enforce, and deleting one user's rows means rewriting files by hand with no record of what\nchanged. This is the \"data swamp\". A data warehouse held curated tables with ACID guarantees and SQL\nperformance, but in a proprietary format, at a higher cost, and poorly suited to images, PDFs, embeddings\nor the Python-first tooling ML teams use. So data was copied from the lake to the warehouse, the copies\ndrifted, and governance had to be enforced twice.\n\nAI workloads make the swamp problem worse. A RAG pipeline wants to append millions of chunk rows while a\nretrieval evaluation reads a consistent snapshot; a training job must be reproducible against exactly the\ndata it saw; a GDPR request requires deleting one person's documents and proving the deletion also reached\nthe derived tables. Each of these needs transactions and versioning on top of cheap storage. The\nlakehouse idea, stated plainly, is: keep the files open and the storage cheap, and add a metadata layer\nthat turns a set of files into a versioned, transactional table that any engine can read.","how":"### Layer 1: open columnar files in object storage\nData lives as Parquet files on S3, GCS or ADLS. Parquet is columnar and compressed, split into row groups\nwith per-column statistics (min, max, null count), so an engine scanning for `tenant_id = 'acme'` can skip\nwhole row groups whose statistics exclude the value. Object storage gives durability and essentially\nunlimited capacity at a low price per GB, but with high per-request latency and no in-place updates: a\nfile is written whole or not at all. Every other design choice follows from that constraint.\n\n### Layer 2: the table format\nA table format is a metadata log that says which files make up the table at each version. Delta Lake\nwrites a `_delta_log` of JSON commit entries with periodic Parquet checkpoints; Iceberg writes a tree of\nmetadata files, manifest lists and manifests pointing at data files; Hudi keeps a timeline of commits. In\nall three, a write is: add new data files, then atomically publish a new log entry or metadata pointer\nthat references them. Because publishing is a single atomic operation, readers either see the old version\nor the new one, never a mix, and concurrent writers use optimistic concurrency: each validates that no\nconflicting commit landed since it started, and retries if one did. This is how ACID is achieved without a\ndatabase server sitting in front of the files. The atomic publish still needs one primitive from somewhere:\na put-if-absent write in the object store (which S3 lacked until it added conditional writes, so Delta on S3\nlong relied on a single writer or an external lock table), or an atomic pointer swap in the catalog, which\nis how Iceberg commits.\n\nHere is one commit with a conflict. The table is at version 41. Writer A appends chunks for new\ndocuments. Writer B runs a GDPR delete that rewrites three files. Both read version 41, write their new\nParquet files (which no reader can see yet, because no log entry references them), and try to publish\nversion 42. A's put-if-absent of entry 42 succeeds. B's fails, so B reads entry 42 and checks whether\nA's changes conflict with what B read and rewrote. A only added files, and if those files cannot hold\nrows matching B's delete predicate (judged by partition values or file statistics, so the check can be\nconservative), there is no conflict and B publishes the same change as version 43. If A had instead\nrewritten one of the files B removed, B would have to abort and re-run against the new state. This is\noptimistic concurrency. It is cheap when writers touch disjoint data and degrades into retry storms when\nmany writers rewrite the same partitions, and that is the condition under which you serialize writers\nthrough one job instead.\n\nThe same log gives you:\n\n- **Snapshot isolation and time travel.** A reader pins a version; a training job can record the\n  snapshot id it read, and reading it again later returns identical rows, as long as that snapshot has not\n  been expired and its files vacuumed. Default retention windows are days to weeks (Delta's default vacuum\n  threshold is 7 days), so a snapshot needed for a year must be explicitly retained (an Iceberg tag, a\n  longer retention setting on that table) or materialized as its own dataset.\n- **Schema enforcement and evolution.** The current schema is in the metadata; writes with a mismatched\n  schema are rejected, and adding a column is a metadata change rather than a rewrite.\n- **Deletes and updates.** Copy-on-write rewrites the affected files and commits the replacement;\n  merge-on-read records deletes in small side files (Iceberg delete files, Delta deletion vectors) that\n  readers apply at scan time, deferring the rewrite to a later compaction. Copy-on-write favours readers,\n  merge-on-read favours frequent small writes.\n- **Partitioning and clustering.** Rows are laid out by a partition value (date, tenant) and optionally\n  clustered within partitions so that statistics-based skipping works. Iceberg's hidden partitioning\n  derives the partition from a column transform so queries need not know the layout.\n\n### Delta, Iceberg and Hudi: what actually differs\nAll three implement the same idea, and they differ mainly in where they came from. Delta Lake came out of\nDatabricks and Spark: a linear log of JSON commits with Parquet checkpoints, most mature inside that\necosystem. Iceberg came out of Netflix as an engine-neutral specification: snapshots point to manifest\nlists, which point to manifests, which point to data files, so planning a query reads a small\nmetadata tree instead of listing directories. It also offers hidden partitioning and partition\nevolution, meaning the layout can change without rewriting old data or changing queries. Hudi came out\nof Uber for upsert-heavy, CDC-fed tables. It offers explicit copy-on-write and merge-on-read table types\nand record-level indexes that locate the file holding a key, which makes frequent upserts cheaper.\nInteroperability layers such as Delta UniForm and Apache XTable write one format's metadata for another\nformat's data, which is why the choice is less permanent than it used to be.\n\n### Layer 3: the catalog\nA catalog maps table names to the current metadata location and is where access control, discovery and\nlineage attach: Hive Metastore, AWS Glue, Unity Catalog, Nessie, or an Iceberg REST catalog. It is the\ncomponent that makes \"the same table\" mean the same thing to Spark, Trino, DuckDB, Flink, Ray and a\npandas script, which is the whole point: no copies, one access policy.\n\n### Maintenance the format does not do for you\nStreaming appends create many small files, and object listing plus per-file overhead then dominates query\ntime; periodic compaction rewrites them into large files. Old snapshots and the files only they reference\naccumulate until expired and vacuumed, which is also the moment a \"deleted\" row is physically gone, so\nretention policy and deletion guarantees depend on running this job.\n\n### Where AI data sits in it\nA common layering is bronze (raw ingested bytes and metadata), silver (parsed, cleaned, conformed) and gold\n(serving-ready tables). For an AI platform the concrete tables are: documents (raw pointer, hash, ACL),\nparsed text, chunks, embeddings (an array column alongside `model_id`), training datasets as pinned\nsnapshots, and evaluation and trace logs. The vector index and any feature-serving store are derived from\nthese tables, rebuilt from them, and never the only copy. The lakehouse is an analytical and batch store:\npoint lookups and sub-100 ms serving belong to Postgres, a key-value store or the vector database.","example":"A chunk table in Delta Lake using the `deltalake` Python package: append new chunks, upsert changed ones by\n`chunk_id`, read an older snapshot for a reproducible evaluation, and compact small files. The same table\ncan be read by Spark, DuckDB or Trino through a catalog.\n\n```python\nimport pyarrow as pa\nfrom deltalake import DeltaTable, write_deltalake\n\npath = \"s3://ai-lake/silver/chunks\"           # or a local directory while developing\n\nbatch = pa.table({\n    \"chunk_id\":   [\"c-001\", \"c-002\"],\n    \"doc_id\":     [\"d-1\", \"d-1\"],\n    \"doc_version\":[\"9f3a\", \"9f3a\"],\n    \"text\":       [\"Refund window is 30 days.\", \"Returns need a receipt.\"],\n    \"model_id\":   [\"emb-v2\", \"emb-v2\"],\n    \"embedding\":  [[0.01, 0.42, -0.13], [0.33, 0.05, 0.27]],   # real width: hundreds to ~3,000 floats\n})\nwrite_deltalake(path, batch, mode=\"append\", partition_by=[\"model_id\"])\n\n# Upsert re-chunked rows: match on chunk_id, update text/embedding, insert new ids.\ndt = DeltaTable(path)\n(dt.merge(source=batch, predicate=\"t.chunk_id = s.chunk_id\", source_alias=\"s\", target_alias=\"t\")\n   .when_matched_update_all()\n   .when_not_matched_insert_all()\n   .execute())\n\n# Reproducible evaluation: pin the snapshot the eval ran against.\neval_version = dt.version()\nfrozen = DeltaTable(path, version=eval_version).to_pyarrow_table()\nprint(eval_version, frozen.num_rows)\n\n# Maintenance: fold small streaming files into large ones, then expire old files.\ndt.optimize.compact()\ndt.vacuum(retention_hours=168, dry_run=False)\n```\n\nWhat the log looks like afterwards shows the mechanism. Version 0 is the append: one `add` action per\nnew Parquet file. Version 1 is the merge. Because these rows already exist, it rewrites the matched file:\na `remove` for the old file and an `add` for its replacement, both in one atomic entry. Version 2 is the\ncompaction: `remove` actions for the small files and an `add` for the large one, with no change to the\nrows. (On this two-row toy table compaction may find nothing to merge and commit nothing; on a real table\nfed by streaming appends it is a large commit.) A reader pinned to version 0 still resolves the original file, because `remove` only drops a file\nfrom later versions and does not delete it from storage. Vacuum is the step that deletes files no\nretained version references.\n\nEvery training or evaluation run that reads this table records `eval_version`; a later audit re-reads the\nsame rows even after the table has moved on, provided the retention policy keeps that version. Note that\n`vacuum(retention_hours=168)` deletes files that only versions older than seven days still reference, so\ntime travel to those versions stops working: retention and reproducibility are one decision, not two.","tradeoffs":["Open files and a shared catalog let any engine read the data without copies, but you now operate compaction, snapshot expiry and vacuuming yourself unless a managed platform does it for you.","Object storage makes capacity nearly free, but per-request latency makes the lakehouse wrong for point lookups or request-path serving; the moment a query needs sub-100 ms, the data needs a derived store.","Copy-on-write keeps reads simple and fast, while merge-on-read makes frequent small updates and deletes cheap; the choice flips toward merge-on-read when CDC-style streams feed the table continuously.","Time travel gives reproducibility and audit cheaply, but every retained snapshot pins files on disk, and a deletion is not a deletion until snapshot expiry runs; long-lived reproducibility and prompt physical deletion pull in opposite directions, so training snapshots that must outlive retention are usually materialized separately with deletion handled explicitly.","Choosing Delta, Iceberg or Hudi is mostly an ecosystem decision (Databricks, Spark and Trino, or a streaming-first stack); the formats converge on features, so engine support and catalog integration should decide."],"pitfalls":["Streaming small appends without compaction; the symptom is queries whose time is dominated by listing and opening thousands of tiny files, visible as low bytes-scanned but long wall-clock.","Partitioning on a high-cardinality column such as `doc_id`; the symptom is millions of directories, slow metadata operations and no skipping benefit.","Treating the lakehouse as the serving database for retrieval or features; the symptom is p99 latency in seconds and a request path that depends on object-store availability.","Storing embeddings without a `model_id` column; the symptom is an index rebuilt from mixed vectors after a model upgrade, with retrieval quality that silently degrades.","Never expiring snapshots or vacuuming; the symptom is storage growing without bound and a GDPR deletion that an auditor can still find in an old version.","Letting every writer evolve the schema freely; the symptom is downstream jobs failing on a renamed column, which the data-contracts lesson addresses."],"when":"Use a lakehouse when more than one engine or team needs the same large tables, when reproducibility and\naudit matter (training sets, evaluation snapshots, deletion proof), or when raw documents and derived\nchunk and embedding tables must coexist with analytics. For a single service with a few gigabytes, a\nPostgres schema is simpler and should win. Measure file count and average file size per table, query bytes\nscanned versus rows returned (skipping effectiveness), snapshot retention versus storage growth, and time\nfrom a deletion request to physical removal. In day-to-day work the lakehouse is where the RAG pipeline's\nstage tables and the training manifest live; in interviews, be ready to explain how an atomic commit over\nimmutable files yields ACID, and why the vector index is derived from a table rather than being the source.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes stage outputs as Delta or Iceberg tables with partition keys and `model_id` columns, pins snapshot versions in evaluation and training configs, and schedules compaction and vacuum jobs.","architect":"Chooses the table format and catalog to match the engines in use, defines the bronze, silver and gold boundaries, and decides which derived serving stores are rebuilt from which tables.","fde":"Lands customer data into the customer's own lakehouse account and region, respects their catalog's access policies, and uses time travel to show exactly which data a delivered model was trained on."},"check":{"q":"A team keeps parsed documents and chunk embeddings as plain Parquet files in S3, written by a nightly\nSpark job and read by a Python evaluation script and a Trino dashboard. They see occasional evaluation\nruns with partially missing data and cannot honour a customer's deletion request confidently. What does\nmoving to a lakehouse table format change, mechanically, and what still remains their responsibility?","a":"Plain Parquet directories have no commit boundary, so the evaluation script sometimes lists files\nwhile Spark is mid-write and reads a mix of old and new. A table format records which files form each\nversion in a log and publishes a new version atomically, so readers see a complete snapshot and writers\ndetect conflicts through optimistic concurrency. Deletion becomes a `DELETE` or `MERGE` that commits a new\nversion excluding the rows, with copy-on-write or deletion files handling the physical layout, and the\nlog records what changed. What stays with the team: the deleted rows persist in prior snapshots until\nexpiry and vacuum run, so a retention job and a check against derived stores such as the vector index are\nstill needed; compaction of the nightly job's small files; and a catalog so Trino, Spark and Python all\nresolve the same table and access policy."},"quiz":[{"q":"How does a lakehouse table format provide atomic commits on object storage that has no in-place updates?","options":["By locking the S3 bucket for the duration of a write","By writing new immutable data files first and then atomically publishing a metadata entry that references them, so readers see the old or new version entirely","By buffering writes in a database server that fronts the files","By using Parquet row-group checksums to detect partial writes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The files themselves are immutable and written whole; the only mutable thing is the pointer or log entry naming the current version. Making that single publish step atomic, with optimistic conflict checks for concurrent writers, yields snapshot isolation without a server in the path."},{"q":"Which workload is a poor fit for a lakehouse and should use a derived store instead?","options":["Rebuilding a vector index from the embeddings table every night","Pinning the exact training set a model run read","Serving per-request vector similarity lookups within a 100 ms latency budget","Running an evaluation across a consistent snapshot while new chunks are appended"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Object storage has high per-request latency and tables are optimized for scans, not point lookups. Request-path retrieval belongs in a vector database or OLTP store that is rebuilt from the lakehouse table, which remains the system of record."},{"q":"After deleting a user's rows from a Delta or Iceberg table, why might an auditor still find them?","options":["Parquet files cannot be modified, so deletes are impossible","The catalog caches query results for 24 hours","Prior snapshots still reference the old files until snapshot expiry and vacuum physically remove them","Merge-on-read tables never apply deletes to readers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Time travel works precisely because old files are retained. A delete commits a new version without the rows, but the previous version remains readable until its files are expired, so the retention job is part of the deletion guarantee."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/document-processing-pipelines","sources":["Official docs: PyMuPDF","Official docs: Docling","Official docs: Unstructured"],"why":"The first parser most teams reach for is a single library call that returns a string per page. It works\non the sample PDFs and breaks on the corpus. Scanned PDFs have no text layer, so the call returns empty\nstrings and the pipeline happily embeds nothing. Two-column papers come out with the lines of both columns\ninterleaved. Tables flatten into a run of numbers with no column association, so \"what was Q3 revenue\"\nretrieves a chunk where the right number sits next to the wrong label. Every page contributes its header\nand footer, so a chunker produces thousands of chunks that begin with \"Confidential — Page 14 of 62\".\nHyphenated line breaks split words. Encoding glitches turn ligatures into garbage. And none of this fails\nloudly: an embedding model will produce a perfectly valid vector for a garbled chunk, so the damage\nappears months later as retrieval that \"sometimes misses obvious passages\".\n\nThe opposite naive approach, sending every page to a vision-capable LLM, fixes most of the quality\nproblems and creates a cost and latency problem: it is orders of magnitude more expensive per page than\nnative text extraction (as an illustrative order of magnitude, somewhere between a tenth of a cent and a\nfew cents per page depending on the model, versus milliseconds of CPU time), it is slow, and it is\nnon-deterministic, so a re-run produces slightly different text and therefore different chunks and\nembeddings. Neither extreme is a pipeline. The pattern is to classify, route, verify and escalate, so that\nthe expensive path is spent only where the cheap path demonstrably failed. The rag-retrieval parsing\nlesson covers the extractors themselves (OCR, layout, tables); this lesson covers the system around them.","how":"### 1. Intake and classification\nIdentify the real type by magic bytes, not extension; customers rename files. Record page count, file\nsize and, for PDFs, whether pages carry a text layer: a page with an image covering the page and near\nzero extractable characters is a scan. Detect language, because OCR engines need the right language\npacks and the quality heuristics below (dictionary-word ratios, hyphen repair) are language-specific. Detect encrypted or malformed files early and park them.\n\n### 2. Routing tiers\nOrder extractors by cost and by what they preserve, and route each document to the first tier that is\nexpected to succeed:\n\n- **Native extraction with layout heuristics** (PyMuPDF, pdfplumber, python-docx, HTML parsers) for\n  born-digital files. Fast, deterministic, cheap; recovers reading order from glyph positions reasonably\n  well and tables poorly.\n- **OCR** (Tesseract, cloud OCR services) for scans and images, producing text with per-word confidence\n  and bounding boxes. Quality depends on resolution, skew and language; preprocessing (deskew, binarize)\n  matters as much as the engine.\n- **Layout-aware document models** (Docling, Unstructured's high-resolution strategy, LayoutLM-family\n  models) that detect regions (title, paragraph, table, figure, caption) and reconstruct table structure.\n  Slower and heavier, but they are what makes multi-column and table-heavy documents usable.\n- **Vision LLMs** for the residue: handwritten forms, charts that need describing, pages where the layout\n  model's output fails verification. Most expensive, non-deterministic, so used last and cached.\n\nThe reason for the ladder is that cost per page differs by orders of magnitude between tiers while most\npages in a typical corpus are born-digital text that tier one handles well.\n\nRoute at the level of the *page* where the format allows it. Real files are mixed: a born-digital\nreport with a scanned appendix, a slide deck where one slide is a screenshot of a table. A per-document\ndecision has to choose one tier for the whole file, which either loses the odd pages or overpays for\nthe ordinary ones. Some decisions must stay at document level, because they need several pages at once:\nheader and footer detection, tables that continue across a page break, and section hierarchy. These\nrun after the per-page extraction, over the assembled blocks.\n\n### 3. The canonical document model\nEvery tier emits the same structure: a list of blocks, each with `type` (title, heading with level,\nparagraph, list item, table, figure, caption, header, footer, footnote), `text`, `page`, `bbox`, and a\n`parent` reference giving section hierarchy. Tables are stored as cell grids with row and column spans\nand additionally serialized to Markdown or HTML for embedding. Figures carry captions and optionally an\nLLM-written description. This model exists so that chunkers, metadata extractors and citation rendering\ndepend on one schema rather than on each extractor's quirks, and so that a document re-processed by a\nbetter tier later slots into the same downstream pipeline.\n\n### 4. Cleaning\nHeaders and footers are found by text that repeats at the same position across many pages and are\ndropped. Hyphenation at line ends is repaired. Unicode is normalized (NFKC), control characters removed,\nwhitespace collapsed while paragraph boundaries are kept. Boilerplate (cookie banners, navigation) is\nstripped for HTML. Each of these is cheap and each removes a class of chunk-level noise that would\notherwise be embedded thousands of times.\n\n### 5. Quality scoring and escalation\nThe output is scored before it is accepted: characters per page against an expectation for the document\ntype, OCR mean confidence, fraction of tokens that are dictionary words, ratio of single-character\n\"words\", presence of known garbage patterns (replacement characters, repeated glyphs). A score below\nthreshold routes the document to the next tier or, after the last tier, to a human review queue. This\ngate is the mechanism that keeps extraction failures from becoming silent retrieval failures.\n\n### 6. Output, versioning and isolation\nThe canonical JSON is stored next to the raw bytes with `parser_tier` and `parser_version`, so an upgrade\nto the layout model can re-process only documents parsed by the old version or the lower tier. Parsers\nrun in isolated workers with timeouts and memory limits, because a malformed PDF can hang or exhaust a\nprocess, and a failure is recorded per document rather than failing the batch. An evaluation set of\ndocuments with gold text and gold tables (measured by character error rate and table cell accuracy) is\nrun on every parser change.","example":"A per-page router with a text-layer check, a quality score and escalation. The extractors are stand-ins\nfor the real tiers that take one page and return canonical blocks; the control flow is the point.\n\n```python\nimport fitz, re                                   # PyMuPDF\n\nMIN_CHARS_PER_PAGE, MIN_WORD_RATIO = 200, 0.85\n\ndef quality(blocks):                              # English prose heuristic; tables scored separately\n    text = \" \".join(b[\"text\"] for b in blocks if b[\"type\"] not in (\"header\", \"footer\", \"table\"))\n    tokens = text.split()\n    if not tokens:\n        return 0.0\n    wordlike = sum(1 for t in tokens if re.search(r\"[A-Za-z]{2,}\", t))\n    garbage = text.count(\"\\ufffd\") + len(re.findall(r\"(.)\\1{5,}\", text))\n    return (wordlike / len(tokens)) * (0.0 if garbage > 20 else 1.0)\n\ndef process(path, tiers):\n    \"\"\"tiers: ordered list of (name, extract_page) returning canonical blocks for one page.\"\"\"\n    pages, errors = [], []\n    for page in fitz.open(path):\n        native = page.get_text().strip()\n        if not native and not page.get_images():\n            pages.append({\"page\": page.number, \"tier\": \"blank\", \"blocks\": []}); continue\n        start = 0 if len(native) >= MIN_CHARS_PER_PAGE else 1   # no usable text layer: skip native\n        for name, extract in tiers[start:]:\n            try:\n                blocks = extract(page)                          # worker timeout in production\n            except Exception as e:                              # a bad page must not kill the batch\n                errors.append((page.number, name, repr(e))); continue\n            if (q := quality(blocks)) >= MIN_WORD_RATIO:\n                pages.append({\"page\": page.number, \"tier\": name, \"quality\": q, \"blocks\": blocks})\n                break\n        else:\n            pages.append({\"page\": page.number, \"tier\": \"needs_review\", \"blocks\": []})\n    return {\"pages\": pages, \"errors\": errors}\n\n# tiers = [(\"native\", native_page), (\"ocr\", ocr_page), (\"layout\", layout_page), (\"vlm\", vlm_page)]\n```\n\nWalk a real document through it: a 60-page born-digital contract whose last 8 pages are a scanned,\nsigned annex. The 52 text pages pass the native tier at near-zero cost. The 8 annex pages have no text\nlayer, so they start at OCR. Suppose 2 of them are handwritten and fail the word-ratio gate; they\nescalate through layout to the vision model. The document costs 52 native pages, 8 OCR pages and 2\nvision-LLM pages. A per-document router would have done worse either way. It would have sent the whole\nfile to native extraction, because 87 % of pages have text, and silently lost the annex. Or, with a\nstricter threshold, it would have OCR'd 52 pages that already had perfect text. The cost argument\nscales. With an illustrative vision-model price of one cent per page, a 1-million-page corpus costs\nabout $10,000 per full pass when every page goes to the vision model, and about $500 when only the 5 %\nthat fail the cheaper tiers do. Re-processing after a parser change multiplies whichever figure you\nchose.\n\nEach record is stored with the raw file's hash and per-page tiers, and `needs_review` pages are counted\non the pipeline dashboard rather than silently indexed as empty. The word-ratio score is deliberately\ncrude and English-specific. It excludes table blocks, because a correct financial table is mostly\nnumbers and would otherwise fail the gate; tables need their own check (consistent column counts per\nrow, numeric cells that parse). Header and footer removal runs after routing, across all pages, because\nit depends on text repeating from page to page.","tradeoffs":["Tiered routing keeps average cost near the cheapest extractor, but it adds a scoring step and a second pass for escalated documents; for a corpus that is almost entirely scans, start at the OCR tier.","Layout models and vision LLMs recover tables and reading order that native extraction loses, at a cost and latency that make them wrong for the whole corpus until retrieval evals prove the gain on your document types.","A rich canonical model with bounding boxes and hierarchy enables precise citations and structure-aware chunking, but it is more work to populate from simple sources such as plain HTML or email.","Deterministic extractors make re-runs reproducible, which vision LLM output is not; cache LLM extractions by page hash and treat them as versioned artifacts to recover reproducibility."],"pitfalls":["Trusting the file extension; the symptom is parser crashes on files that are secretly HTML, images or zip archives, and documents classified as processed that produced nothing.","Not detecting scanned PDFs; the symptom is documents in the index with zero or one chunk, found when a user asks about a contract that \"should be there\".","Skipping header and footer removal; the symptom is chunks that begin with a confidentiality notice and a page number, and retrieval that favours documents with the longest boilerplate.","Flattening tables to text; the symptom is numeric questions answered with the right number from the wrong column or year.","No parser version in the output; the symptom is an inability to re-process only the documents an old, buggy extractor touched, so every parser fix becomes a full re-run.","Routing whole documents by an average text-layer ratio; the symptom is a contract whose scanned signature annex is missing from the index while the rest of the document retrieves fine.","Running parsers in the orchestrator's process; the symptom is one malformed file hanging or OOM-killing an entire nightly batch."],"when":"Reach for a tiered pipeline as soon as a corpus mixes formats or includes scans, tables or multi-column\nlayouts, which describes nearly every enterprise document set. A homogeneous corpus of Markdown or HTML\nneeds only the cleaning and canonical-model steps. Measure text-layer ratio, per-tier volume and cost,\nquality-score distribution, escalation rate, review-queue size, and extraction accuracy on the gold set;\nescalation rate climbing is the earliest sign of a new document type entering the corpus. Day to day this\nis where retrieval incidents are traced to and fixed; in interviews, \"how would you ingest 50,000 mixed\nPDFs for RAG\" should be answered with classification, routing by cost, a canonical structure, quality\ngates and selective re-processing by parser version, not with the name of a library.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements classification, the extractor adapters that emit the canonical block model, header and footer removal, and the quality score, and runs the gold-set accuracy check on every change.","architect":"Defines the canonical document schema and the tier ladder with its cost and quality thresholds, and decides where vision LLM extraction is allowed given cost, latency and data policy.","fde":"Profiles the customer's real documents in the first week, finds the scans and the table-heavy forms the demo never saw, and sets expectations about which document classes need review or custom handling."},"check":{"q":"A customer's RAG system answers well for policy documents but fails on their financial reports,\nwhich are multi-column PDFs with many tables; some older reports are scans. The current pipeline uses one\ntext-extraction library for everything. Describe how you would restructure processing and how you would\nknow it worked.","a":"First, classify: detect the scans by text-layer ratio and send them to OCR with deskewing instead of\nnative extraction, which currently yields nothing for them. Second, route the born-digital reports to a\nlayout-aware extractor that reconstructs reading order across columns and emits tables as cell grids,\nserialized to Markdown for embedding, with the original cells kept for citation. Third, define a canonical\nblock model so both routes produce the same structure, remove repeated headers and footers, and store\nparser tier and version with each output. Fourth, add a quality score with escalation to a vision LLM for\npages that fail, cached by page hash. To know it worked: build a small gold set from the failing reports\nwith hand-verified text and table cells, measure character error rate and cell accuracy before and after,\nand re-run the retrieval evaluation on the questions that used to fail, expecting the numeric questions to\nretrieve the correct table chunk."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why route documents through tiers rather than sending every page to the best available extractor?","options":["The best extractors cannot read born-digital PDFs","Cost and latency per page differ by orders of magnitude across tiers, and most pages are handled well by the cheapest one, so escalation should be driven by measured failure","Vision LLMs are not permitted to process documents in most jurisdictions","Layout models require a text layer to operate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A typical corpus is mostly born-digital text where native extraction is accurate and nearly free. A quality gate identifies the minority of pages that need OCR, layout analysis or a vision model, keeping spend proportional to difficulty."},{"q":"What is the purpose of a canonical document model shared by all extractors?","options":["It compresses documents for cheaper storage","It lets the vector database index documents without chunking","It decouples chunking, metadata and citation logic from each extractor's output format, so documents can be re-processed by a better tier without downstream changes","It guarantees deterministic output from vision LLMs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Downstream stages should depend on one schema of typed, ordered blocks with hierarchy and tables as cells. Then swapping or upgrading an extractor, or escalating a document to a higher tier, changes nothing after the parsing stage."},{"q":"A quality gate flags a batch of documents with chars-per-page near zero after native extraction. What does this most likely indicate?","options":["The documents are written in a language the chunker does not support","The pages are scanned images without a text layer and need OCR","The embedding model rejected the text as too short","The headers and footers were removed too aggressively"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Native extraction reads the PDF's text layer; scans have none, so the output is empty even though the pages are full of content. The gate exists precisely to catch this and route the documents to OCR instead of indexing them as empty."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/vector-database-architecture","sources":["Malkov & Yashunin, 2018 — Efficient and robust approximate nearest neighbor search using Hierarchical Navigable Small World graphs","Subramanya et al., 2019 — DiskANN: Fast Accurate Billion-point Nearest Neighbor Search on a Single Node","Official docs: Qdrant","Official docs: pgvector"],"why":"Exact nearest-neighbour search is a dot product per stored vector. For a million 1,536-dimensional\nfloat32 vectors that is about 6 GB of reads per query, which at the tens of GB/s of memory bandwidth a\nserver offers is on the order of 100 ms or more per query, and it scales linearly with the corpus. Approximate indexes such as HNSW cut that to a few thousand distance\ncomputations by navigating a graph, which is why ANN indexes exist and the rag-retrieval module explains\nthem. But an index alone is a library call, not a database. Production retrieval also needs to add and\ndelete vectors while serving queries, attach and filter on metadata (tenant, date, ACL), survive a process\nrestart, replicate for availability, and spread across machines when the corpus outgrows one.\n\nEach of those requirements collides with the index. HNSW builds a graph whose quality depends on\ninsertion order and whose nodes cannot simply be removed without leaving the graph disconnected; deletes\nbecome tombstones and rebuilds. A filter applied after the top-k is returned yields fewer than k, or zero,\nresults when the filter is selective, and a filter applied before means searching a set the graph was not\nbuilt for. Graph traversal performs random memory access, so an index that spills to disk becomes orders\nof magnitude slower unless it was designed for SSDs. Sharding a similarity search means every shard must\nanswer and the results must be merged. A vector database is the engineering that resolves those\ncollisions; the choice between a dedicated engine and a vector extension inside Postgres or OpenSearch is\na choice about which of them you need solved and which you can live with.","how":"### Data model\nA collection holds points: an id, one or more named vectors (dense, and in many engines sparse for\nhybrid search), and a payload of key-value metadata. Payload fields that will be filtered on get\nsecondary indexes (keyword, numeric range, geo), typically inverted or bitmap indexes, because filter\nevaluation must produce sets of candidate ids quickly.\n\n### Index layer and why it is memory-bound\nHNSW keeps a layered proximity graph; a query enters at the top layer, greedily walks to the nearest\nnode, descends, and at the base layer explores a beam of `ef` candidates. Each hop reads a node's\nneighbour list and its vector, from unpredictable addresses, so throughput tracks memory latency and the\nindex must be RAM-resident. IVF partitions vectors into clusters by k-means and scans a few posting lists,\nwhich is more sequential and cheaper in memory but needs training and loses recall on skewed data.\nQuantization (scalar int8, product quantization, binary) shrinks the vectors the index touches during\ntraversal, trading a small recall loss that is recovered by rescoring the final candidates against\nfull-precision vectors kept on disk. DiskANN-style designs push this further: a compressed copy in RAM\nguides the walk, and full vectors plus adjacency live on SSD with one read per visited node, which is how\nbillion-scale search fits on one machine.\n\n### Write path: segments, tombstones and compaction\nWrites do not go into the graph directly. The common design, shared in spirit by Qdrant, Milvus and\nLucene-based engines, is: append to a write-ahead log for durability; insert into a small mutable segment\nthat is searched by brute force; when it reaches a threshold, seal it and build an index in the\nbackground; serve queries across all segments and merge. Deletes mark a tombstone bitmap consulted at\nquery time, and updates are a delete plus an insert. Periodic compaction merges segments, drops\ntombstoned points and rebuilds the graph. This design exists because it keeps writes cheap and the index\nimmutable, at the cost of eventual visibility (a point is searchable after it lands in the mutable segment,\nwhich may be after a refresh interval) and of query cost that creeps upward between compactions as\ntombstones accumulate and segment counts grow.\n\n### Query path and the filtering problem\nA query is: embed (outside the database), evaluate the filter to a candidate set, search, merge across\nsegments and shards, rescore if quantized, fetch payloads, return top-k. The filter strategy is the\nsubtle part.\n\n- **Post-filtering:** run ANN for top-k′ (k′ > k), then drop points failing the filter. Simple, and\n  wrong when the filter is selective: if 1 % of points match, top-100 contains on average one survivor.\n- **Pre-filtering:** compute the allowed-id bitmap from the payload index, then search only among them.\n  Correct, but a graph traversal restricted to 1 % of nodes becomes disconnected, so engines fall back to\n  brute force over the allowed set when it is small, which is fine precisely because it is small.\n- **Filter-aware traversal:** check the filter at each hop and keep exploring through disallowed nodes\n  without returning them, with extra graph links built per indexed payload field so the subgraph stays\n  connected (Qdrant's approach), or a flat-search cutoff below which the engine abandons the graph\n  (Weaviate's `flatSearchCutoff`; newer Weaviate releases also offer an ACORN-style filtered traversal). pgvector historically returned fewer than k rows under a selective `WHERE`\n  because the index scan ran first; newer releases added iterative index scans that keep walking until\n  enough rows pass.\n\nA plain `WHERE tenant_id = ?` across millions of points is therefore an architecture question, not a\nsyntax question, and it is the first thing to test when evaluating an engine.\n\n### Distribution and multi-tenancy\nSharding by hashed point id spreads load evenly but makes every query scatter to all shards and merge,\nso latency is the slowest shard's. Sharding by a routing key such as tenant sends each query to one\nshard, which is both faster and a natural isolation boundary, at the cost of hot shards for large tenants.\nReplication provides availability and read scaling; most engines offer per-request consistency levels\nbecause replicas apply writes asynchronously. Multi-tenancy has three patterns: a collection per tenant\n(strong isolation, but each HNSW graph carries fixed overhead and thousands of tiny collections waste\nmemory), a payload filter per tenant (cheap, relies on the filtered-search path and on nobody forgetting\nthe filter), and tenant-partitioned indexes where the engine builds a separate graph per tenant value\ninside one collection, which is the usual recommendation for many small tenants.\n\n### Dedicated engine versus extension\npgvector gives ANN inside the database that already holds the chunks, with transactions, joins and row\nsecurity, and it is the right answer until the index no longer fits comfortably in the database's memory\nor filtered recall and write throughput become the bottleneck. OpenSearch and Elasticsearch give HNSW\nbeside BM25 and existing log infrastructure. Dedicated engines win on filtered-search machinery,\nquantization options, segment management and horizontal scaling. The architecture above is the lens for\ndeciding which of those you will actually hit.","example":"The filtering problem, made visible. This simulation uses exact similarity, so the only variable is\n*when* the filter is applied. One collection holds three tenants owning 90 %, 9.5 % and 0.5 % of the\npoints. Post-filtering takes the top-k′ by similarity and then drops other tenants' points.\nPre-filtering builds the allowed-id set first and scans it exactly.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nN, D, K = 200_000, 64, 10\nX = rng.standard_normal((N, D)).astype(np.float32)\nX /= np.linalg.norm(X, axis=1, keepdims=True)              # cosine = dot product\nshare = {\"big\": 0.90, \"mid\": 0.095, \"small\": 0.005}         # tenant sizes: 90 %, 9.5 %, 0.5 %\ntenant = rng.choice(list(share), size=N, p=list(share.values()))\n\ndef post_filter(q, t, k_prime):                             # ANN-style: top-k' first, then filter\n    top = np.argpartition(-(X @ q), k_prime)[:k_prime]\n    return [i for i in top if tenant[i] == t][:K]\n\ndef pre_filter(q, t):                                       # allowed ids first, exact scan over them\n    allowed = np.flatnonzero(tenant == t)\n    scores = X[allowed] @ q\n    return allowed[np.argsort(-scores)[:K]], len(allowed)\n\nq = rng.standard_normal(D).astype(np.float32); q /= np.linalg.norm(q)\nfor t in share:\n    got = [len(post_filter(q, t, kp)) for kp in (10, 100, 1000)]\n    ids, scanned = pre_filter(q, t)\n    print(f\"{t:>5}: post-filter results for k'=10/100/1000 -> {got}; \"\n          f\"pre-filter -> {len(ids)} results, scanned {scanned:,} vectors\")\n# Output (seed 0):\n#   big: post-filter results for k'=10/100/1000 -> [9, 10, 10]; pre-filter -> 10 results, scanned 179,893 vectors\n#   mid: post-filter results for k'=10/100/1000 -> [1, 10, 10]; pre-filter -> 10 results, scanned 19,110 vectors\n# small: post-filter results for k'=10/100/1000 -> [0, 0, 4]; pre-filter -> 10 results, scanned 997 vectors\n```\n\nRead the output as the planner's decision table. Post-filtering returns about `k′ × selectivity`\nsurvivors: fine for the big tenant, and empty for the small one even at k′ = 1,000, a hundred times the\nrequested k. Pre-filtering is always correct, but its cost is the size of the allowed set. Now scale to a\nproduction collection of 10 million 1,024-dimensional float32 vectors (4 KB each). The small tenant's\n50,000 vectors are about 200 MB to scan exactly, a few milliseconds to a few tens of milliseconds on one\nnode depending on memory bandwidth and threads. The big tenant's 9 million are about 36 GB, which is far\ntoo much per query, so for it the engine must walk the graph and check the filter during traversal,\nwhere the filter hardly prunes anything. A good engine estimates the allowed-set size from the payload\nindex and picks per query: exact scan below a threshold (Qdrant's `full_scan_threshold`, Weaviate's\n`flatSearchCutoff`), filter-aware graph search above it. When you evaluate an engine, test the tenant\nsizes in between, where neither strategy is cheap.\n\nIn a real engine the setup is a payload index on the filtered field, for example\n`client.create_payload_index(\"chunks\", field_name=\"tenant\", field_schema=\"keyword\")` in Qdrant, plus a\nfilter passed with the query. A delete marks a tombstone and returns at once; its memory comes back when\nthe segment is optimized. Neither behaviour shows on a toy collection, because servers brute-force\nsegments below their indexing threshold. Benchmark at realistic sizes.","tradeoffs":["HNSW gives high recall at low latency but costs RAM for links plus vectors and a slow build; IVF or disk-based indexes flip the choice when the corpus is billions of vectors or memory is the budget line.","Quantization cuts index memory by 4× to 32× and often speeds traversal, at a recall cost that rescoring mostly recovers; it stops being worth it when the collection is small enough to fit unquantized anyway.","Pre-filtering is correct for selective filters but expensive when the filter matches most of the collection; engines pick per query by estimated selectivity, and you should test both extremes.","Tenant-routed sharding gives isolation and single-shard queries, but a tenant larger than one shard forces a hybrid of routing plus scatter-gather that most engines handle less gracefully.","A vector extension in an existing database saves a system and gives transactions with the chunk tables, but it flips toward a dedicated engine when filtered recall, write throughput or memory residency in the shared database become the bottleneck."],"pitfalls":["Post-filtering with a selective filter; the symptom is queries that return fewer than k results, or none, for small tenants while large tenants work fine.","Measuring recall only on a freshly built index; the symptom is retrieval quality and latency drifting worse over months as tombstones and segment fragmentation accumulate without compaction.","Sizing memory for vectors alone and forgetting graph links, payload and merge headroom; the symptom is OOM kills during background index builds rather than during steady-state queries.","Storing large payloads such as full chunk text inside the vector engine; the symptom is RAM dominated by text while the index itself is small, and slow snapshots and rebalances.","Relying on application code to add the tenant filter; the symptom is a cross-tenant result in a log or a security review, which the ACL-aware retrieval lesson treats as a structural problem.","Expecting writes to be immediately searchable; the symptom is an integration test that upserts and queries in the same millisecond and intermittently fails."],"when":"Think in these terms whenever you choose or tune a vector store, size a cluster, or debug retrieval that\nis correct in notebooks and wrong in production. The questions to answer are concrete: how selective are\nthe filters you will run, how many writes and deletes per hour, does the index fit in RAM with headroom,\nhow many tenants and how skewed are they, and what recall at what p99 latency. Benchmark filtered recall\nand latency at your real selectivity, not the vendor's. Recall has a precise meaning here: take a few\nhundred real queries with their real filters, compute the exact top-k by brute force, and report the\nfraction of those ids the engine returns, at the p99 latency you get with the same settings. Repeat\nafter a month of writes and deletes, because a freshly built index is the best case. Start with pgvector when the chunk tables already\nlive in Postgres and the corpus is in the low millions; move to a dedicated engine when the architecture\nabove says one of its mechanisms is what you need. In interviews, explaining why a selective filter\nbreaks naive ANN and how segments and tombstones make deletes eventual is the level expected of an\narchitect.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Creates payload indexes for every filtered field, sets `ef` and quantization per collection, tests filtered recall at real selectivity, and schedules or monitors compaction.","architect":"Chooses between an extension and a dedicated engine, defines the sharding and multi-tenancy pattern, and sets recall, latency and freshness targets the engine must be benchmarked against.","fde":"Maps the customer's tenant, ACL and retention requirements onto the engine's filtering and isolation model, and demonstrates that a deleted document stops being retrievable within the agreed window."},"check":{"q":"A SaaS product serves 3,000 tenants from one collection using a payload filter on `tenant_id`.\nSmall tenants complain that search returns only one or two results, large tenants report slow queries,\nand a security audit asks how cross-tenant leakage is prevented. Explain the mechanisms behind each\nsymptom and propose an architecture.","a":"Small tenants get few results because the filter is applied after similarity search: the engine\ntakes the top-k′ over all 3,000 tenants and then discards the others, so a tenant owning 0.1 % of points\ngets about `k′ × 0.001` survivors, which is effectively none. Large tenants are slow for related reasons.\nThe usual workaround for small tenants, a larger k′ or `ef` on every query, makes each query walk much\nmore of the shared graph. And a large tenant's points are spread over every shard, so each of its\nqueries pays scatter-gather and waits for the slowest shard. Leakage prevention currently depends on\napplication code remembering the filter. The fix has three parts. First, give the engine what it needs\nto plan by selectivity: a payload index on `tenant_id`, and ideally per-tenant index structure inside the\ncollection (in Qdrant, a tenant-marked keyword index with graphs built per payload value instead of one\nglobal graph). Small tenants are then searched exactly or as small graphs, and none of them is a sliver\nof a huge one. Second, put the handful of very large tenants on dedicated shards or collections, which\ngives them isolation and predictable latency. Third, enforce the filter in a retrieval service that\nderives `tenant_id` from authentication, never from the request body. Then verify with recall@k against\nexact search, measured separately at the smallest, median and largest tenant sizes."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does an HNSW index need to be resident in RAM to perform well?","options":["Because the distance computation uses SIMD instructions that require RAM","Because graph traversal reads neighbour lists and vectors from unpredictable addresses, so each hop is a random access that disk cannot serve quickly","Because the graph is rebuilt on every query","Because payload filters are stored in the same memory region"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A query walks tens to thousands of nodes, each at an address determined by the previous hop. Random reads from SSD are orders of magnitude slower than from memory, which is why disk-based designs like DiskANN keep a compressed copy in RAM to guide the walk and read full vectors once per visited node."},{"q":"A deletion in a vector database returns instantly but memory is not freed. What explains this?","options":["The engine batches deletes until the next replica sync","Deletes are only applied to the payload store, not the vectors","The point is marked with a tombstone consulted at query time, and the graph and memory are rebuilt during a later compaction","Vector indexes cannot remove nodes, so deletes are ignored"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Removing a node from a proximity graph would leave neighbours disconnected and degrade recall, so engines mark deletions and filter them out of results. Compaction later rebuilds segments without the tombstoned points, which is when memory and query cost recover."},{"q":"With a filter that matches 0.5 % of a collection, which filtering strategy is likely to return correct top-k results?","options":["Retrieve the top-k by similarity, then discard points failing the filter","Compute the matching ids first and search only within them, falling back to brute force over that small set","Keep post-filtering but double the candidate count k′ before discarding non-matching points","Disable the HNSW index and rely on the payload index alone"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With 0.5 % selectivity, a top-100 similarity result contains about half a matching point on average, and doubling k′ to 200 still yields about one, so post-filtering returns almost nothing. Pre-filtering yields a small candidate set that is cheap to scan exactly, which is why engines switch strategy based on estimated selectivity."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/vector-store-sizing","sources":["Jégou et al., 2011 — Product Quantization for Nearest Neighbor Search","Kusupati et al., 2022 — Matryoshka Representation Learning","Official docs: hnswlib","Official docs: Qdrant"],"why":"Teams size vector stores the way they size nothing else: by picking a managed tier that \"looks big\nenough\" or an instance with the most RAM they can justify. Both fail. Under-sizing shows up as\nout-of-memory during a background index build, p99 latency jumping when the index spills out of memory,\nor a managed plan that silently caps vectors so the last 30 % of the corpus never lands. Over-sizing\nshows up as a five-figure monthly bill for a corpus that would fit in a Postgres replica.\n\nThe inputs are not mysterious. Vector count is documents times chunks per document, and chunks per\ndocument follows from chunk size and overlap. Bytes per vector is the embedding dimension times the\nstorage precision. Index overhead is a function of the graph parameters. Payload is whatever text and\nmetadata you decided to keep in the engine. What makes the estimate wrong in practice is omission: counting\ndocuments instead of chunks (a 10× error), forgetting that the ANN graph needs memory of its own, storing\nthe full chunk text beside each vector and discovering text dominates, or forgetting that a re-embedding\nmigration doubles the collection for its duration. This lesson is the checklist and the arithmetic, so\nthat the number comes from the corpus rather than from a pricing page.","how":"### Step 1: count vectors\n`vectors = documents × chunks_per_document × vectors_per_chunk`. With token-based chunking,\n`chunks_per_document ≈ tokens_per_document / (chunk_tokens − overlap_tokens)`, rounded up per document.\n`vectors_per_chunk` is 1 for standard dense retrieval, more than 1 if you embed multiple views (title plus\nbody, or a summary) and much more for late-interaction models that store one vector per token. Add the\ngrowth rate: a corpus adding 2 % per month is 27 % larger in a year.\n\n### Step 2: bytes per vector\n`dim × bytes_per_dim`: float32 is 4 bytes per dimension, float16 is 2, scalar int8 quantization is 1, and\nbinary quantization is 1 bit (one eighth of a byte). Product quantization compresses to a fixed number of\nbytes per vector, commonly tens of bytes. Dimension comes from the model: common text embedding models\nsit between 384 and 3,072 dimensions, and Matryoshka-style models let you truncate to a smaller prefix\nat a quality cost you must measure. Quantized indexes usually keep the full-precision vectors on disk for\nrescoring, so disk is not freed, only RAM.\n\n### Step 3: index overhead\nHNSW stores neighbour lists. With degree parameter `M`, the base layer holds up to `2M` links per node\nand each link is a 4-byte id, so link storage is about `2 × M × 4` bytes per vector plus a small amount\nfor upper layers; `M = 16` gives roughly 128 bytes per vector. The hnswlib parameter guide puts the\nlink memory at roughly `M × 8–10` bytes per element, which matches this arithmetic; engines add their own\nper-point bookkeeping (ids, versions, locks) on top, so treat it as a lower bound. IVF adds only centroids and posting\nlists of ids, which is negligible next to the vectors.\n\n### Step 4: payload and secondary indexes\nEverything stored beside the vector costs memory in most engines: the id, filterable fields and their\nindexes, and the chunk text if you keep it there. Text is the surprise: at roughly 4 characters per token\n(an English-prose approximation), a 400-token chunk is about 1.6 KB, larger than an int8-quantized\n1,024-dimensional vector. If memory is the constraint, keep text and rich metadata in Postgres or object\nstorage keyed by `chunk_id` and store only ids and filter fields with the vector.\n\n### Step 5: replicas and headroom\nMultiply by the replication factor for availability. Then add headroom: segment merges and index rebuilds\ntemporarily hold two copies of a segment, and operating at the edge of RAM turns every compaction into an\nincident. A factor of about 1.5 is a common rule of thumb. Note what it is applied to: Qdrant's capacity\nguidance multiplies raw vector bytes by 1.5 to cover index structures, metadata and temporary segments\nduring optimization together, whereas this lesson itemizes links and payload first and then applies 1.5×\nas build headroom, which is the more conservative reading. Treat either as a floor, not a target.\n\n### Step 6: throughput and shards\nMemory gives one lower bound on node count; queries per second give another. Per-node throughput depends\non dimension, `ef`, filter selectivity and hardware, so it must be measured with your own vectors and\nfilters, not taken from a benchmark. `nodes = max(ceil(total_RAM / RAM_per_node), ceil(peak_QPS /\nQPS_per_node))`, sized at peak rather than average, within the latency budget the product allows retrieval.\n\n### Step 7: decide what must be RAM-resident, then size the disk\nNot every byte has to be in RAM, and the line between \"must be\" and \"may be\" is set by access pattern.\nThe graph links and whatever vectors the traversal compares against are read at random on every hop,\nso they must be resident; if they are memory-mapped from disk and the working set falls out of the page\ncache, latency rises by orders of magnitude. Full-precision vectors used only for rescoring are read a\nfew dozen to a few hundred times per query (k times the oversampling factor), so on an SSD they can live\non disk. Payload is fetched only for the final k. Engines expose this as per-collection settings (for\nexample on-disk vectors or payload in Qdrant, with the quantized copy kept in RAM). Then size the disk\nseparately: full-precision vectors, the quantized copy, the write-ahead log, and snapshots or backups,\nwhich are complete copies. Disk is cheap but not free, and the snapshot is what you restore from.\n\n### Step 8: migrations\nA change of embedding model means the new vectors coexist with the old until cutover. Budget for the\ncollection to double during that window, or plan to migrate tenant by tenant so the overlap is bounded;\nthe re-indexing lesson in this module covers the strategy.","example":"A worked scenario, then a calculator you can keep.\n\nA knowledge base has 200,000 documents averaging 4,000 tokens. Chunks are 400 tokens with 50 overlap, so\neach document yields about 4,000 / 350 ≈ 11.4 chunks; with rounding per document call it 12, giving\n2.4 million vectors. The embedding model is 1,024-dimensional.\n\n- Vectors in float32: 2.4 M × 4,096 B ≈ 9.8 GB.\n- HNSW links at `M = 16`: 2.4 M × 128 B ≈ 0.3 GB.\n- Payload with chunk text (≈ 1.6 KB) plus ids and filter fields (≈ 0.4 KB): 2.4 M × 2 KB ≈ 4.8 GB.\n- Steady state per replica ≈ 14.9 GB; with 1.5× headroom ≈ 22 GB; two replicas ≈ 45 GB of RAM in total.\n\nNow change two decisions. Scalar int8 quantization makes the vectors 2.4 M × 1,024 B ≈ 2.5 GB, so the\nin-memory total becomes roughly 2.5 + 0.3 + 4.8 ≈ 7.6 GB, and text is now the biggest line. Move text out\nto the chunk table and keep 200 B of ids and filters: 2.5 + 0.3 + 0.5 ≈ 3.3 GB, about 5 GB with headroom\nper replica. The same corpus went from needing large-memory nodes to fitting on one modest node, with\nfull-precision vectors (9.8 GB) kept on disk for rescoring. Whether a given engine can do this exact\nconfiguration varies: pgvector, for instance, offers half-precision (`halfvec`, 2 bytes per dimension) and\nbinary (`bit`) types rather than int8, so its nearest equivalent is about 4.9 GB of vectors, still small\nenough for an existing database host. All figures are illustrative order-of-magnitude arithmetic; measure\nrecall after quantizing.\n\nPush one step further to see the next bottleneck. Binary quantization stores 1,024 bits, which is 128\nbytes per vector, so the vectors take 2.4 M × 128 B ≈ 0.3 GB. That is the same as the HNSW links. From\nhere, shrinking vectors further barely helps, because the graph and the per-point ids and filter fields\nnow dominate. The remaining levers are a smaller `M` (fewer links, lower recall), or IVF-style or\ndisk-based indexes whose overhead is not per-link. Binary codes also lose more recall than int8, so they\nare normally used with heavier oversampling and rescoring from full-precision vectors on disk. Each\nlever you pull exposes the next term of the formula as the largest one.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef vector_store_bytes(docs, tokens_per_doc, chunk_tokens, overlap, dim,\n                       bytes_per_dim=4.0, M=16, payload_bytes=2048, replicas=2, headroom=1.5):\n    chunks_per_doc = math.ceil(tokens_per_doc / (chunk_tokens - overlap))\n    n = docs * chunks_per_doc\n    vec = n * dim * bytes_per_dim\n    links = n * 2 * M * 4                      # base-layer HNSW neighbour ids\n    payload = n * payload_bytes\n    per_replica = (vec + links + payload) * headroom\n    return {\"vectors\": n, \"vector_gb\": vec / 1e9, \"links_gb\": links / 1e9,\n            \"payload_gb\": payload / 1e9, \"ram_per_replica_gb\": per_replica / 1e9,\n            \"cluster_ram_gb\": per_replica * replicas / 1e9}\n\nbase  = vector_store_bytes(200_000, 4_000, 400, 50, 1024)\nlean  = vector_store_bytes(200_000, 4_000, 400, 50, 1024, bytes_per_dim=1.0, payload_bytes=200)\nfor name, r in ((\"float32 + text in engine\", base), (\"int8 + ids only\", lean)):\n    print(f\"{name}: {r['vectors']:,} vectors, {r['ram_per_replica_gb']:.1f} GB per replica, \"\n          f\"{r['cluster_ram_gb']:.1f} GB cluster\")\n```\n\nRun it with your own corpus numbers before any vendor conversation; the two scenarios print about 22.6 GB\nand 4.9 GB per replica, a factor of roughly 4.6 in RAM for the same retrieval corpus.","tradeoffs":["Quantization cuts RAM by 4× (int8) to 32× (binary) and speeds traversal, but costs recall that must be measured per model; for a collection that fits unquantized with headroom, it is complexity for nothing.","Keeping chunk text in the engine makes retrieval a single call and simplifies the stack, but text often outweighs quantized vectors; move it out when memory, snapshot time or rebalance time are the constraint.","Larger chunks mean fewer vectors and a smaller index, but retrieval precision usually falls as chunks grow; size for the chunking the retrieval evals chose, not the other way round.","Reducing dimension (a smaller model or a Matryoshka prefix) shrinks memory linearly, at a quality cost that is small for some models and tasks and large for others; it is a measured decision, not a default.","More replicas buy availability and read throughput at linear cost; a single replica with a fast rebuild from the chunk table is acceptable for internal tools and wrong for a customer-facing SLA."],"pitfalls":["Counting documents instead of chunks; the symptom is a cluster sized for 200,000 vectors receiving 2.4 million and failing to load the index.","Forgetting graph links and merge headroom; the symptom is out-of-memory kills during background compaction or index rebuild rather than under query load.","Ignoring payload size; the symptom is RAM dominated by text with an index that is a fraction of it, and slow snapshots and rebalances.","Sizing throughput on average QPS; the symptom is p99 latency collapsing during the daily peak while dashboards show healthy averages.","Assuming a spill to disk is a graceful degradation; the symptom is latency rising by orders of magnitude once the graph no longer fits, because traversal is random access.","Not budgeting for the migration window; the symptom is a model upgrade blocked because the cluster cannot hold both embedding versions at once."],"when":"Do this arithmetic whenever a vector store is being chosen, a tier is being upgraded, a chunking or\nembedding change is proposed, or a tenant is being onboarded with a known corpus. Measure the real\nchunks-per-document distribution from a sample rather than assuming the mean, measure recall before and\nafter any quantization, and load-test QPS with production-shaped filters. The number tells you whether\npgvector in the existing database suffices, whether quantization is worth its recall cost, and what the\nmanaged-service bill will be at the chosen precision. In interviews, \"how big a vector store does this\ncorpus need\" is answered by walking the eight steps with explicit assumptions and then naming the two\nlevers, quantization and payload placement, that change the answer most.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Computes vectors, bytes and overhead from the pipeline's real chunk statistics, chooses quantization and payload placement, and verifies recall and peak QPS on a staging cluster.","architect":"Owns the capacity model and the cost line for retrieval, decides between an extension and a dedicated engine on the basis of the estimate, and budgets memory for re-embedding migrations.","fde":"Sizes a customer deployment from their document count and page statistics during scoping, and explains why the quoted tier changes when they add a second content source or a larger embedding model."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants to index 1.5 million support tickets averaging 300 tokens with a 1,536-dimensional\nfloat32 embedding, one chunk per ticket, on a managed service, and they want the full ticket text\nreturned with each hit. Estimate the memory, then show how you would cut it by more than half without\nchanging the embedding model or the retrieval results they see.","a":"One chunk per ticket gives 1.5 million vectors. Float32 at 1,536 dimensions is 6,144 bytes per\nvector, so about 9.2 GB of vectors; HNSW links at `M = 16` add about 128 bytes each, roughly 0.2 GB; ticket\ntext at 300 tokens is about 1.2 KB plus metadata, roughly 2 GB. Steady state is about 11.4 GB, around\n17 GB with 1.5× headroom per replica, and double that for two replicas. To cut it: store the vectors with\nscalar int8 quantization, bringing vectors to about 2.3 GB while keeping float32 copies on disk for\nrescoring so returned results are effectively unchanged, and move ticket text out of the engine into the\nticket database keyed by id, fetched after retrieval. The in-memory total drops to roughly 2.3 + 0.2 +\nsmall metadata, under 3 GB before headroom, which is a quarter of the original. Then measure recall on a\nlabelled query set to confirm quantization did not change the top-k, and load-test at peak QPS."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which omission most often causes a vector store to be under-provisioned by an order of magnitude?","options":["Forgetting that cosine similarity needs normalized vectors","Counting documents rather than chunks when estimating the number of vectors","Using float16 instead of float32 in the estimate","Ignoring the number of HNSW layers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A document with a few thousand tokens becomes ten or more chunks, each with its own vector. Estimates that start from document count miss this multiplier entirely; precision and graph layers change the total by much smaller factors."},{"q":"After enabling int8 scalar quantization, why does disk usage not fall (it even grows slightly) while vector RAM drops roughly 4×?","options":["The engine compresses the payload instead of the vectors","Quantization only applies to the HNSW links","The quantized copy replaces float32 in memory, but full-precision vectors are kept on disk for rescoring the final candidates","Disk usage is always dominated by the write-ahead log"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Quantization trades precision during graph traversal for memory, and engines recover accuracy by re-ranking the shortlist against the original vectors. Those originals must still exist, so disk holds both copies while RAM holds only the small one."},{"q":"Why should the capacity estimate include a headroom factor beyond steady-state bytes?","options":["Because object storage charges per request","Because embedding dimensions grow over time","Because segment merges and index rebuilds temporarily hold additional copies, and a cluster at the edge of RAM fails during those operations","Because replicas must each hold the full write-ahead log"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Background maintenance builds a new segment or graph before releasing the old one. Without free memory for that overlap, compaction and re-indexing trigger out-of-memory failures even though steady-state queries fit."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","sources":["Kusupati et al., 2022 — Matryoshka Representation Learning","Official docs: pgvector","Official docs: Qdrant"],"why":"Every RAG index silently depends on three things: which model turned text into vectors, how that model was\ncalled (dimension, normalization, instruction prefix, input truncation) and how documents were cut into\nchunks. None of those appear in the vector itself. A query embedded with model B against an index built\nwith model A does not return \"slightly worse\" results. If the dimensions differ, the store at least\nrejects the query; if they happen to match, cosine similarity between vectors from different spaces is\nnoise, so retrieval degrades to near-random while the pipeline reports no error at all.\n\nThis is forced on you, not optional. Embedding models improve and vendors deprecate old ones; you will\nwant longer context, a multilingual model, or a cheaper one; you will tune the chunker; you may quantize\nvectors to save memory. Each of those invalidates every stored vector.\n\nThe naive alternative is \"just re-embed in place\": point the ingestion job at the new model and overwrite\nrows. It fails three ways. During the hours or days the job runs, the index contains a mix of two spaces and\nlive queries hit both. If the new model turns out worse on your eval set, there is nothing to roll back to.\nAnd it re-embeds unchanged content, paying full cost for a corpus where usually only a small fraction\nchanged since the last run. Versioning the index and swapping it as a unit removes all three problems.","how":"### What makes up an embedding version\nTreat the index as a product of a recipe and give the recipe a version identifier:\n\n```\nembedding_version = hash(model_id, model_revision, dimension, normalization,\n                         instruction_prefix, max_input_tokens, chunker_id, chunker_params)\n```\n\nStore this on every vector row and on the collection itself, *because* the only reliable way to know\nwhether a query is compatible with an index is to compute the query-side recipe and compare. The query\npath reads the collection's version and refuses, or routes elsewhere, on mismatch. Model *revision*\nmatters, not just name: hosted models can be silently updated, and open-weight models on a hub have\ncommit hashes. Pin them.\n\nChunker parameters belong in the recipe because retrieval granularity changes what a vector means. A\n512-token chunk and a 128-token chunk of the same paragraph are different items even under the same\nmodel, and the citation offsets you return to users change with them.\n\n### Re-embedding versus re-indexing\nTwo different operations hide behind the word \"re-index\", and they cost very different amounts.\n*Re-embedding* produces new vectors because the recipe changed; it is the expensive path described\nabove. *Re-indexing* rebuilds only the ANN structure over the vectors you already have, and it is needed\neven when the model never changes:\n- **Parameter changes.** HNSW's `M` and `ef_construction` are fixed when the graph is built, so raising\n  them to buy recall means a rebuild. (`ef_search` is a query-time knob and needs no rebuild, which is why\n  it is the first thing to tune.)\n- **Centroid staleness.** IVF-style indexes assign each vector to the nearest of a set of centroids learned\n  from the data present at build time; pgvector's documentation advises creating an IVFFlat index only\n  after the table holds data for this reason. As the corpus grows into topics the original sample did not\n  cover, lists become unbalanced and recall at a fixed `nprobe` falls, so the centroids must be retrained.\n- **Accumulated deletes and updates.** Many implementations mark deleted vectors rather than removing\n  them, so a heavily churned index carries dead entries that cost memory and can weaken graph\n  connectivity until compaction or rebuild.\n\nRe-indexing uses the same blue-green swap but skips the embedding calls, so it takes hours of index\nbuild rather than days of API throughput. Keep the two version counters separate (`embedding_version`\nfor the vectors, an index build id for the structure) so you can tell which one moved when recall changes.\n\n### The query side is part of the version\nThe alias must resolve to a *pair*: the collection and the recipe that built it. The query path reads the\nrecipe from the alias target and embeds the query with exactly that model, prefix and dimension, *because*\nswapping the collection while the query embedder stays on the old model recreates the cross-space\nmismatch in reverse. Any cache of query embeddings must therefore be keyed on\n`(query_text, embedding_version)`, never on text alone.\n\nA learned mapping from the new model's space into the old one (a linear or small adapter trained on pairs\nof embeddings of the same texts) is sometimes used as a bridge, letting a new query model run against the\nold index before the rebuild finishes. It recovers only part of the quality, so treat it as a temporary\nmeasure that must pass the same eval gate, not as an alternative to rebuilding.\n\n### Content hashing to avoid re-embedding the unchanged\nGive each chunk a stable key: `chunk_key = hash(source_id, chunk_text)`. Keep a table mapping\n`(chunk_key, embedding_version) -> vector`. On each ingestion run, chunk the source, compute keys and\nskip any key already embedded under the current version. Chunks whose text changed get new keys and are\nembedded; keys that disappear are deleted. This works *because* embedding is, for practical purposes, a\npure function of (text, recipe): the same inputs yield the same vector up to small floating-point noise\nfrom batching and hardware, so a cache keyed on both is safe. When the\nembedding version changes, every key is a miss and you pay for a full rebuild, which is correct.\n\n### Blue-green indexes and the alias swap\nBuild the new version into a separate collection or table (`docs_v7` beside `docs_v6`) while the old one\nkeeps serving. Most stores support an alias or a pointer the application resolves at query time (Qdrant\ncollection aliases, OpenSearch index aliases; in pgvector a view or a config row naming the live table).\nWhen the build finishes and passes evaluation, repoint the alias in one operation. Queries never see a\nmixed index, rollback is repointing the alias back, and the old collection is deleted after a soak\nperiod. The price is temporarily holding two copies of vectors and two ANN graphs, which must be planned\ninto memory and disk sizing.\n\n### Catching up on writes during the build\nDocuments keep changing while the rebuild runs. Two patterns: *dual-write*, where ingestion embeds every\nnew chunk under both versions until the swap; or *replay*, where the build records its start watermark,\nand after finishing, re-processes everything changed since. Embedding cost is roughly the same either\nway, since each changed chunk is embedded once per version; replay is slightly cheaper when the same\ndocument changes several times during the window, because only its final state is embedded for the new\nversion. The real difference is operational. Dual-write keeps the new collection current at every moment\nbut means the backfill and live writes race on the same collection (a backfill must not overwrite a newer\nlive write, and deletes must reach both). Replay needs a reliable change watermark and a final catch-up\npass, so there is a short lag to close just before the swap.\n\n### Evaluate before you swap\nA new model is not better by assumption. Run the retrieval eval set (queries with known relevant chunks)\nagainst both collections and compare recall@k and MRR. Also check the downstream answer quality on a\nsample, because a model that retrieves different-but-also-relevant chunks can shift answers in ways\nretrieval metrics miss. The swap is gated on this comparison, which is why the build must be separate.\n\n### Dimension and quantization changes\nChanging dimension (for example truncating a Matryoshka-trained model to fewer dimensions) or storage\nprecision (float32 to int8 or binary) changes the stored representation and the ANN index parameters; it\nis a new embedding version even if the model is the same. Rebuild rather than convert in place, and\nre-run the eval, because quantization costs recall that must be measured, not assumed.\n\n### Cost and duration (illustrative arithmetic)\nA corpus of 10 million chunks averaging 400 tokens is 4 billion tokens. At an illustrative hosted price of\na few cents per million tokens that is on the order of a hundred dollars, which is rarely the problem. The\nconstraint is throughput: at an illustrative rate limit of a million tokens per minute the embedding\ncalls alone take roughly 67 hours, before HNSW build time. That duration is what makes dual-write or\nreplay necessary and makes content hashing worth having for the routine runs in between.","example":"A minimal version of the three mechanisms: a recipe hash, a content-keyed embedding cache, and an alias\nswap. The vector store is abstracted as a dict of collections so the logic is visible.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json\n\n# \"revision\" is your own pin; hosted APIs may not expose one, so record the date you validated it\nRECIPE = {\"model\": \"text-embedding-3-small\", \"revision\": \"2024-01\", \"dim\": 1536,\n          \"normalize\": True, \"chunker\": \"recursive\", \"chunk_tokens\": 400, \"overlap\": 40}\nEMB_VERSION = hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(RECIPE, sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest()[:12]\n\ndef chunk_key(source_id: str, text: str) -> str:\n    return hashlib.sha256(f\"{source_id}\\x00{text}\".encode()).hexdigest()[:16]\n\ncollections = {}                 # name -> {chunk_key: (vector, meta)}\nalias = {\"docs\": \"docs_v6\"}      # what queries resolve against\ncache = {}                       # (chunk_key, EMB_VERSION) -> vector\n\ndef embed(text):                 # stand-in for the model call\n    return [float(b) for b in hashlib.md5(text.encode()).digest()[:4]]\n\ndef build(target: str, sources: dict[str, list[str]]):\n    col = collections.setdefault(target, {})\n    seen, calls = set(), 0\n    for sid, chunks in sources.items():\n        for text in chunks:\n            k = chunk_key(sid, text); seen.add(k)\n            if (k, EMB_VERSION) not in cache:\n                cache[(k, EMB_VERSION)] = embed(text); calls += 1\n            col[k] = (cache[(k, EMB_VERSION)], {\"source\": sid, \"emb_version\": EMB_VERSION})\n    for k in set(col) - seen:    # chunks that vanished upstream\n        del col[k]\n    return calls\n\ndocs = {\"kb/1\": [\"Refunds take 5 days.\", \"Contact support via chat.\"]}\nprint(\"first build, calls:\", build(\"docs_v7\", docs))          # 2\ndocs[\"kb/1\"][0] = \"Refunds take 3 days.\"\nprint(\"incremental, calls:\", build(\"docs_v7\", docs))          # 1 (only the changed chunk)\n\ndef swap(name, new_target):      # gate this on the eval comparison in real life\n    old, alias[name] = alias.get(name), new_target\n    return old\nprint(\"retired:\", swap(\"docs\", \"docs_v7\"), \"live:\", alias[\"docs\"])\n```\n\nThe eval gate is deliberately a comment: in production `swap` runs only after recall@k on the eval set for\n`docs_v7` is at least as good as for `docs_v6`.","tradeoffs":["Blue-green rebuilds give atomic cutover and instant rollback but double vector and index memory during the build; if the corpus barely fits one GPU or one node, you need a staged or tiered rebuild instead.","Content hashing makes routine runs cheap, but a hash on exact text means a one-character edit re-embeds the chunk and its overlapping neighbours, and with fixed-size chunking an insertion shifts every later boundary in that document, so all following chunks re-embed too; structure-aware chunking that cuts on headings or paragraphs keeps the blast radius local.","Pinning a model revision protects consistency but means you consciously own upgrades; letting a vendor update silently is cheaper to operate right up until retrieval quality shifts with no deploy to blame.","Dual-write keeps the new index current with no catch-up step but couples live ingestion to the new collection and makes it race the backfill; replay keeps ingestion untouched at the cost of a catch-up pass, and tends to win when builds take days and the change watermark is trustworthy.","Keeping old versions for rollback is safe but each held version is storage and an ANN graph; set an explicit retirement window rather than letting versions accumulate."],"pitfalls":["Upgrading the query-side model without rebuilding the index; the symptom is retrieval that returns plausible-looking but irrelevant chunks with no errors and eval recall that collapses overnight.","Changing the chunker and reusing the old vectors because \"the model is the same\"; symptom is citations pointing at wrong offsets and duplicated or truncated passages in context.","Re-embedding in place against the live collection; symptom is a window of mixed-space results and user complaints that correlate with the ingestion job's schedule.","Swapping on build completion rather than on eval results; symptom is a regression discovered by users, with the old index already deleted.","Forgetting that filters and metadata live beside vectors; a rebuild that drops ACL or tenant fields produces an index that passes retrieval eval and fails a security review.","Sizing memory for one copy of the index; symptom is out-of-memory or evictions precisely during the rebuild, the moment you can least afford it.","Caching query embeddings keyed on query text alone; the symptom after a swap is that popular (cached) queries retrieve garbage while rare, uncached queries work fine.","Treating every recall drop as a model problem; when recall decays slowly with no recipe change, the cause is usually stale IVF centroids or accumulated deletes, and a re-index (no re-embedding) fixes it."],"when":"Reach for explicit versioning from the first production index, because the first model upgrade arrives\nsooner than planned and the retrofit is painful. The alias-swap pattern is the default for any corpus that\ntakes longer than a few minutes to rebuild; below that, a rebuild-and-replace in a transaction is fine.\nContent hashing pays for itself as soon as ingestion runs on a schedule rather than once. Measure four\nthings: fraction of chunks re-embedded per run (should be small except on version changes), build duration\nversus the rate of upstream change (decides dual-write versus replay), eval recall before and after\nevery swap, and recall on a fixed eval set tracked weekly between swaps, since a slow decline with no\nrecipe change is the signal to re-index rather than re-embed. In interviews, the expected answer to \"how do you upgrade the embedding model in production\"\nis this exact sequence: version the recipe, build beside, evaluate, swap the alias, retire later.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Stamps every vector with the recipe hash, writes the content-keyed embedding cache, and wires the alias lookup into the query path so mismatches fail loudly.","architect":"Owns the capacity plan that includes two live indexes during rebuilds and the policy for how many versions are retained and for how long.","fde":"Explains to a customer why a \"quick model upgrade\" is a multi-day rebuild with an eval gate, and designs the replay window around their document change rate."},"check":{"q":"Your team wants to move a 20-million-chunk index from a 1536-dimension model to a newer\n1024-dimension model. Walk through the plan and the two biggest risks.","a":"First, define the new recipe (model id, pinned revision, 1024 dims, normalization, same chunker if\nchunking is unchanged) and hash it as a new embedding version. Build it into a new collection while the\nold serves; estimate duration from token count divided by the embedding throughput limit, and because\nthat is likely days, either dual-write new chunks under both versions or record a watermark and replay\nchanges afterward. Run the retrieval eval set against both collections and compare recall@k and MRR,\nplus a sample of end-to-end answers. Swap the alias only if the new one is at least as good, keep the old\ncollection for a rollback window, then delete it. The two biggest risks are memory: both collections and\nboth ANN graphs coexist, so the cluster must have headroom; and silent quality regression: the new model\nmay retrieve differently on your domain, which only the eval gate catches."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can't you query an index built with embedding model A using vectors from model B?","options":["Model B produces vectors that are too large to compare efficiently","The two models define unrelated vector spaces, so similarity between their vectors is meaningless","Model B normalizes vectors differently, which the index can correct for at query time","The ANN index stores the model name and rejects the query"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each model learns its own geometry; the same text lands at unrelated points. Cosine similarity across spaces is noise, which is why retrieval degrades to near-random, with no error when the dimensions happen to match. Indexes check dimension at most, never which model produced a vector, unless you store and check the version yourself."},{"q":"What is the main purpose of keying the embedding cache on both chunk content hash and embedding version?","options":["To allow the same vector to be reused across different embedding models","To detect duplicate documents across sources","To skip re-embedding unchanged chunks on routine runs while forcing a full rebuild when the recipe changes","To make vector lookups faster at query time"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Embedding is a pure function of (text, recipe). Keying on both means an unchanged chunk under the same recipe is a cache hit, and any change to model, dimension or chunker makes every key miss, which is the correct behaviour."},{"q":"During a multi-day rebuild, documents keep changing. Which approach keeps the new index current without a mixed-space window?","options":["Pause ingestion until the rebuild finishes","Write new chunks only to the old index and copy them over after the swap","Dual-write new chunks under both versions, or record a watermark and replay changes after the build","Embed new chunks with whichever model is faster and tag them"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Dual-write or watermark-and-replay both deliver a complete new collection at swap time. Pausing ingestion leaves users with stale data for days; copying after the swap creates a gap; mixing models in one collection is exactly the failure versioning exists to prevent."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/knowledge-management","sources":[],"why":"Point a crawler at a company wiki, a ticketing system and a shared drive, embed everything, and you get\nan index where the question \"what is the refund window\" matches a 2019 policy page, a 2023 revision, a\ndraft that was never approved, a Slack export of someone guessing, and three copies of the same PDF with\ndifferent file names. The retriever ranks them by similarity, which has nothing to do with correctness or\ncurrency. The model then synthesizes an answer from a mixture and the output is confidently wrong.\nImproving the embedding model, the reranker or the prompt does not fix this: all of them operate on\ncandidates, and the candidates are the problem.\n\nThe naive alternative is \"index more, the model will sort it out\". It fails because the model has no\nsignal that one chunk is authoritative and another is deprecated unless that signal is carried in the\ndata. Larger context windows make it worse, not better: more contradictory passages fit, and the model's\nchoice between them is arbitrary.\n\nThe second naive alternative is a one-time cleanup. It fails because corpora drift continuously: pages are\nedited, products are renamed, people leave and their documents go unmaintained. Without ownership and\nlifecycle rules, the cleaned corpus decays back within months. Knowledge management turns a one-off\ncleanup into a standing process with owners, metadata and feedback loops.","how":"### Decide what is canonical\nFor each class of question, name the system of record. Pricing comes from the catalogue, not from sales\ndecks that quote it. Policy comes from the policy repository, not from support macros that paraphrase it.\nThis matters *because* retrieval needs a way to break ties between similar passages, and \"which source is\nauthoritative for this topic\" is the tiebreaker humans use. Encode it as a `source_rank` or `authority`\nfield on every chunk so the retriever or reranker can prefer canonical sources and the generator can cite\nthem. Secondary sources are still useful for recall, but they should lose to primaries on conflict.\n\n### Assign ownership and freshness expectations\nEvery indexed source gets an owner (a team, not a person who may leave) and a review cadence. The owner's\njob is to confirm the content is still correct or to retire it. Record `last_verified_at` separately from\n`last_modified_at`: a document untouched for two years may be perfectly current, and one edited yesterday\nmay be a typo fix on an obsolete page. Freshness scoring in retrieval should use verification, not\nmodification, where the distinction exists.\n\n### Give documents a lifecycle\nModel the states explicitly: draft, published, deprecated, archived. Only published content enters the\nindex by default. Deprecated content either leaves the index or carries a flag that the prompt surfaces\n(\"this page is marked superseded by X\"). Archived content is removed. The lifecycle lives in the source\nsystem where possible (a wiki label, a status field) and is read by ingestion, *because* content owners\nwork in the source tool, not in the vector database, and a state they cannot see will not be maintained.\n\n### Deduplicate and canonicalize\nNear-duplicates waste context slots and make the retriever look confident when it has really found the\nsame passage three times. Exact duplicates are caught by content hashing at ingestion. Near-duplicates\n(the same PDF exported twice with different headers) need a similarity threshold or a MinHash pass, and a\nrule for which copy survives, usually the one in the canonical source. Keep the mapping so that\nretrieval hits on the discarded copy can be redirected to the surviving one.\n\n### Decide what belongs in the index at all\nNot every fact should be embedded. An index is a snapshot refreshed on the ingestion cadence, so any fact\nthat changes faster than that cadence, or that must be exact for this user right now (a price, stock\nlevel, account balance, ticket status), is stale by construction once indexed. Those facts belong in the\nsystem of record and are fetched at query time through a tool or API call; the index holds the stable\nprose that explains them (how pricing tiers work, what a status means). The rule of thumb: index\nexplanations, look up values. This is *because* a retrieved chunk carrying last week's price is\nindistinguishable to the model from a correct one, while a live lookup either returns the current value or\nfails visibly.\n\n### Keep a controlled vocabulary\nProducts get renamed, teams reorganise, and three departments call the same thing three names. If tags\nare free text, a filter on `product = \"Atlas Pro\"` misses every page still tagged with last year's name.\nMaintain a small glossary that maps aliases and former names to one canonical term, apply it to tags at\ningestion, and use it for query expansion at retrieval time. It is cheap, it is owned like any other\nsource, and it fixes a class of misses that no embedding model reliably closes, because a new product\nname often has no semantic relationship to the old one.\n\n### Carry metadata that the retriever can act on\nAt minimum: source system, owner, authority rank, lifecycle state, verified date, audience or access\nscope, product or domain tags, and language. These are what metadata filters and rerankers use to turn\n\"similar\" into \"relevant to this user now\". Metadata that is not populated at ingestion is never\npopulated, so the ingestion contract should reject documents missing required fields rather than\ndefaulting them.\n\n### Close the loop from failures to content\nRetrieval failures are the best signal about the corpus. When users thumbs-down an answer or an eval\nquery misses, classify the cause: missing document, stale document, duplicate crowding out the right\none, or a genuine retrieval failure. Route the first three to the content owner with the query attached.\nThis is the mechanism that keeps the corpus healthy over time, and it is cheap once ownership exists.\nWithout it, the same gap is rediscovered by every user who hits it.\n\nTwo equally authoritative sources that disagree are a special case worth detecting explicitly. When the\ntop candidates share an authority rank but contradict each other on a value, the better behaviour is to\nshow the user both with their dates and open a ticket for the owners, rather than let the model pick one\nsilently, because the silent pick is right only by chance and nobody learns the conflict exists.\n\n### Access and tenancy are part of knowledge, not an afterthought\nA document's permissions are knowledge about who may see it. Ingestion must capture ACLs from the source\nand retrieval must filter on them; a corpus that is correct but leaks HR pages to engineering is a\nfailed deployment. Treat access scope as a required metadata field like any other.","example":"A worked scenario with illustrative numbers. An internal support assistant for a software company is built\nover three sources: the product documentation site (about 4,000 pages), the support team's Confluence\nspace (about 9,000 pages accumulated over six years) and a Google Drive folder of PDFs (about 2,500\nfiles). Everything is chunked and embedded; the eval set of 300 support questions scores 58 percent\n\"answer correct\" when judged by the support leads.\n\nFailure analysis on the 126 wrong answers finds: 51 cite a Confluence page that was superseded by a newer\none still in the index; 23 blend numbers from a PDF export of an old price list with the current docs;\n19 retrieve near-identical chunks from three copies of the same onboarding guide, pushing the actually\nrelevant page out of the top five; 14 answer from an unapproved draft; 19 are genuine retrieval or\ngeneration problems.\n\nThe team does not change the model. They declare the documentation site canonical for product behaviour\nand the pricing catalogue canonical for prices, and give both `authority=1`; Confluence gets\n`authority=2`. Confluence pages get a lifecycle label; the support leads spend two afternoons marking 3,100\npages deprecated and 800 archived, which ingestion now respects. Content hashing plus a near-duplicate\npass removes about 1,900 chunks. Price-list PDFs are excluded entirely because the catalogue API is now\ningested directly. The reranker is given `authority` and `last_verified_at` as features.\n\nRe-running the eval gives 81 percent correct (243 of 300), so the content fixes recovered 69 of the 107\ncontent-caused failures. Of the 57 that remain, 19 are the genuine retrieval or generation problems, which\nnow get attention as a clean, isolated bucket. The rest are content issues the first pass missed (pages\nsuperseded but not yet labelled, near-duplicates below the dedup threshold) and questions whose answers\nexist nowhere in writing, which are logged as content requests to the docs team. A weekly report now lists deprecated pages still receiving retrieval hits and\nunanswered question clusters, owned by the support leads.","tradeoffs":["Declaring canonical sources improves correctness on conflicts but suppresses useful secondary context; when the canonical source is thin, you may need secondaries to win on recall and lose only on conflict.","Strict lifecycle gating (only published content indexed) keeps the corpus clean but hides work in progress; for engineering teams that live in drafts, indexing drafts with a visible flag can be better.","Aggressive deduplication frees context slots and sharpens ranking, but a threshold set too low merges legitimately different versions, so the choice depends on how much the corpus versions in place.","Ownership and review cadences make the corpus trustworthy, but they cost owner time every cycle; if nobody will do the reviews, a shorter freshness-based demotion is more honest than a fake cadence.","Rich required metadata improves filtering, but every required field is a reason for a document to be rejected at ingestion; keep the required set small and the optional set large."],"pitfalls":["Treating a bad answer as a model problem and iterating on prompts; the symptom is eval scores that move a point or two per week while the same stale pages keep getting cited.","Indexing everything a connector can reach because recall seems free; the symptom is top-k results dominated by duplicates and drafts and a context window full of contradictory passages.","Using modification date as freshness; the symptom is a correct, stable policy page demoted below a recently edited but obsolete one.","Storing lifecycle state only in the vector database; the symptom is owners marking pages obsolete in the wiki while the index keeps serving them because nobody re-ran the cleanup.","Skipping ACL capture at ingestion to ship faster; the symptom is a security finding or a user seeing a page they could never open in the source system.","No feedback path from users to content owners; the symptom is the same gap reported many times with nobody positioned to fix it.","Indexing volatile values such as prices or stock levels instead of looking them up; the symptom is answers that were right on the day of ingestion and drift wrong until the next run."],"when":"Knowledge management is required for any RAG system over content that people maintain, which is nearly\nall of them; it can be skipped only for corpora that are effectively immutable and single-sourced, such\nas a frozen archive of closed case files or a product's own generated logs. Start it before the first index, since the ingestion schema must carry the metadata.\nMeasure: share of eval failures attributed to content versus retrieval versus generation, count of\ndeprecated pages still receiving hits, duplicate ratio in top-k, and median `last_verified_at` of cited\nchunks. In day-to-day work it shows up as the ingestion contract and the weekly content report. In\ninterviews, \"how would you improve a RAG system scoring 60 percent\" should start with failure analysis of\nthe corpus, not with a bigger model.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds ingestion that reads lifecycle and ACL state from source systems, enforces required metadata, deduplicates, and exposes authority and verification fields to the reranker.","architect":"Decides which systems are canonical for which domains, defines the metadata schema every connector must satisfy, and sets the ownership and review model with the business.","fde":"Runs the failure analysis with the customer's subject-matter experts, negotiates who owns which content, and turns \"the bot is wrong\" into a prioritized content backlog."},"check":{"q":"A customer's RAG assistant is scoring poorly, and they want to switch to a larger model. What do you\ndo first, and how do you argue for it?","a":"Before touching the model, sample the failures and classify each by cause: missing document, stale or\nsuperseded document, duplicates crowding out the right chunk, access-filtered content, or a true retrieval\nor generation error. In enterprise corpora the first three often dominate, and no model fixes them because\nthe model only sees the candidates it is given. Show the customer the breakdown with concrete cited pages:\n\"this answer came from a 2021 page that your own wiki marks obsolete\". Then propose the fixes that address\nthe largest buckets: canonical sources with authority ranks, lifecycle labels read by ingestion,\ndeduplication, and a feedback loop to content owners. Re-run the eval after those changes; if genuine\nretrieval or generation errors remain the largest bucket, that is when a model or retriever change is\njustified, and the eval now isolates its effect."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a larger context window not fix a corpus with contradictory documents?","options":["Larger windows are slower, so fewer documents can be retrieved","More contradictory passages fit, and the model has no signal about which one is authoritative","Embedding models cannot embed documents longer than the window","The reranker discards documents beyond the first few thousand tokens"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The problem is the absence of an authority signal, not capacity. Fitting more passages increases the number of conflicting claims the model must choose between, and without metadata indicating which source is canonical, its choice is arbitrary."},{"q":"Which field is the better basis for freshness in retrieval, and why?","options":["last_modified_at, because recent edits indicate active maintenance","last_verified_at, because an owner confirming correctness is different from any edit having happened","created_at, because older documents are more established","indexed_at, because it reflects when the chunk entered the system"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A stable policy page may be unedited for years yet fully current, while an obsolete page may get a typo fix yesterday. Verification captures what freshness actually means; modification only captures activity."},{"q":"Where should a document's lifecycle state (published, deprecated, archived) be maintained?","options":["In the vector database metadata, since that is what retrieval reads","In a spreadsheet maintained by the AI team","In the source system where content owners work, read by ingestion","In the prompt, as a list of deprecated page titles"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Content owners maintain state where they already work. A state stored only in the index or a side spreadsheet drifts immediately because the people who know a page is obsolete never see it. Ingestion propagates the source's state into the index."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/data-lineage","sources":["Official docs: OpenLineage","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications"],"why":"An AI system is a long chain of transformations: source documents become parsed text, text becomes\nchunks, chunks become vectors, vectors join with metadata into an index; separately, raw records become\ncleaned datasets, datasets become training mixes, mixes become checkpoints, checkpoints become served\nmodels. Each hop is written by a different job, often by a different team, and each hop loses information\nabout the one before it. A vector in the index does not know which parser version produced its text; a\nmodel checkpoint does not know which rows were filtered out of its training set.\n\nThen something goes wrong, and the question is always upstream. A customer asks for their data to be\ndeleted: which chunks, which caches, which fine-tuning sets, which checkpoints contain it? Retrieval\nquality drops on Tuesday: did the source change, the parser, the chunker, the embedding model, or the\nindex build? A model produces an output that should have been impossible: was the offending example in\nthe training data, and which pipeline let it through? Each question is \"walk backward from this artifact\nto its inputs\" or \"walk forward from this input to everything derived from it\".\n\nThe naive alternative is reading the pipeline code. It fails because code describes what *could* run,\nnot what *did* run with which inputs at which version. The second alternative, file naming conventions and\nREADME files, fails because they are not updated by the jobs themselves and drift immediately. Lineage\nhas to be emitted by the job at run time, as a side effect of doing the work, or it will not be accurate.","how":"### The graph\nLineage is a directed graph with two node types: **datasets** (a table, a file prefix, a vector\ncollection, a model artifact, a prompt template) and **jobs** (a pipeline step). A **run** is one\nexecution of a job, and it carries edges: run *reads* datasets A and B, run *writes* dataset C. Dataset\nversions are nodes, not just dataset names, *because* \"the training table\" is not an input; \"the training\ntable as of snapshot 2024-06-01T00:00Z, 4.2 million rows, content hash h\" is. Without versioned nodes,\nthe graph tells you a job touched a table and nothing about which state of the table.\n\n### Granularity\nTable-level lineage says job J read table T. Column-level lineage says column `price_usd` in C derives\nfrom `price_cents` in A through J. For AI pipelines a third level is needed: **record-level** or\n**chunk-level** lineage, where each chunk in the index carries the identifier of its source document, the\ndocument version, the parser version and the chunker version. This is heavier, but it is the only\ngranularity at which \"delete this person's data\" and \"why was this passage retrieved\" can be answered.\nThe practical compromise: table-level for the warehouse, record-level for anything that feeds retrieval,\nfine-tuning or a model's outputs.\n\n### Capture at run time, not by declaration\nThe job emits lineage events as it runs: a start event naming the job, run id and inputs with their\nversions; a complete event naming outputs with their versions and facets such as row counts, schema and\nthe code commit. Failed and aborted runs emit events too (OpenLineage defines FAIL and ABORT states),\n*because* a run that wrote half its output before crashing is exactly the one you need to find later. This design is chosen *because* declared lineage (a YAML file describing the DAG) tells\nyou the intended graph, not the actual one for a specific run. OpenLineage is the common open standard for\nthese events; orchestrators and transformation tools (Airflow, Spark, dbt among them) can emit them,\nMarquez is the reference backend that stores and renders the graph, and catalogs such as DataHub can\ningest the events. Other catalogs (Apache Atlas, for example) use their own lineage model, so check\nwhich format your catalog actually consumes. Even without the tooling, a table of `(run_id, job, direction,\ndataset, version, facets)` rows gives you the same power.\n\n### Facets that make lineage useful\nA bare edge is not enough. Each run records the code version (commit hash, container digest), the\nparameters (chunk size, embedding model id), the environment, and per-dataset statistics (row count,\nschema fingerprint, content hash). These are what turn \"the index was rebuilt\" into \"the index was rebuilt\nby commit abc with chunk size 512 from document snapshot h\". When two runs produce different results, you\ndiff their facets.\n\n### Traversals\n- **Impact analysis (forward):** from a dataset version, everything derived from it. Used before changing\n  a source schema and for deletion propagation: a document id walks forward to chunks, vectors, caches,\n  eval sets and fine-tuning examples.\n- **Root cause (backward):** from an artifact, its ancestors. Used when quality regresses: compare the\n  ancestor facets of the good run and the bad run.\n- **Reproduction:** the backward closure plus the code versions is the recipe to rebuild an artifact.\n\n### Lineage versus provenance\nThe two words are often used interchangeably; the useful distinction is scope. Lineage is the whole\nderivation graph across the platform, queried by traversal (\"what depends on this table\"). Provenance is\nthe verifiable record attached to one artifact (\"this checkpoint was built from these digests by this\ncommit, and here is a signature\"). Provenance manifests are nodes and facets in the lineage graph; the\ngraph is what lets you walk from one manifest to every other artifact that shares an input.\n\n### Runtime-derived artifacts are the usual hole\nBatch jobs are easy to instrument because an orchestrator wraps them. The artifacts that escape lineage\nare the ones the application creates on the request path: an agent's memory summary of a conversation, a\nsemantic-cache entry, a generated answer saved as a draft, a user-feedback row copied into an eval set.\nEach is derived data with inputs, and each needs the same treatment: record the ids of the inputs it was\nderived from (conversation, retrieved chunk ids, model version) at write time. Without that, forward\ntraversal from a deleted document stops at the batch boundary and misses the copies users actually see.\n\nNote also where traversal stops being actionable. Forward traversal can tell you that a document reached\na training snapshot and therefore a checkpoint, but it cannot remove the document's influence from the\nweights; it tells you which models must be retrained or retired. Lineage finds the blast radius;\nretention and deletion architecture decides what to do about it.\n\n### Lineage for RAG specifically\nEach chunk stored in the vector index carries `source_id`, `source_version`, `parser_version`,\n`chunker_version`, `embedding_version` and `ingest_run_id`. Each retrieved chunk is then logged with the\nrequest trace, which connects the serving-time trace to the ingestion-time graph. That join is the\nmechanism behind trustworthy citations: the citation is not \"a chunk that looks like page 12\" but \"chunk\nc, from document d at version v, ingested by run r\".\n\n### Lineage for training\nA training run's inputs are dataset versions and a base model version; its outputs are checkpoints and\neval results. Recording the exact data snapshot, as an immutable version that is retained and addressed\nby its hash, is what lets you answer \"was example x in the training set\" by looking it up rather than\nre-running the filter pipeline; a hash on its own proves which bytes were used but cannot be searched,\nso the snapshot must be kept for as long as that question can be asked. It is also what dataset\ncontamination checks need.","example":"A minimal lineage recorder for a RAG ingestion step. It writes versioned dataset nodes and run edges to an\nin-memory store, then answers the two traversals that matter: forward impact from a document and backward\nroot cause from a chunk.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, uuid, time\n\nedges = []          # (run_id, direction, dataset_id)\nruns = {}           # run_id -> facets\nchunk_lineage = {}  # chunk_id -> facets\n\ndef version_of(content: bytes) -> str:\n    return hashlib.sha256(content).hexdigest()[:12]\n\ndef ingest(doc_id: str, text: str, commit: str, chunker=\"recursive-v3\", emb=\"emb-v7\"):\n    run_id = str(uuid.uuid4())[:8]\n    doc_ver = version_of(text.encode())\n    runs[run_id] = {\"job\": \"ingest\", \"commit\": commit, \"chunker\": chunker,\n                    \"embedding_version\": emb, \"started\": time.time()}\n    edges.append((run_id, \"reads\", f\"doc:{doc_id}@{doc_ver}\"))\n    for i, piece in enumerate(text.split(\". \")):            # stand-in chunker\n        chunk_id = f\"chunk:{doc_id}:{doc_ver}:{i}\"\n        chunk_lineage[chunk_id] = {\"source\": f\"doc:{doc_id}@{doc_ver}\", \"run\": run_id,\n                                   \"chunker\": chunker, \"embedding_version\": emb}\n        edges.append((run_id, \"writes\", chunk_id))\n    return run_id\n\ndef forward(dataset_id: str):\n    \"\"\"Everything derived from a dataset version (deletion propagation / impact).\"\"\"\n    out, frontier = set(), {dataset_id}\n    while frontier:\n        d = frontier.pop()\n        for run_id in {r for r, dir_, ds in edges if dir_ == \"reads\" and ds == d}:\n            for r, dir_, ds in edges:\n                if r == run_id and dir_ == \"writes\" and ds not in out:\n                    out.add(ds); frontier.add(ds)\n    return out\n\ndef backward(chunk_id: str):\n    \"\"\"Why does this chunk look the way it does?\"\"\"\n    lin = chunk_lineage[chunk_id]\n    return {**lin, \"run_facets\": runs[lin[\"run\"]]}\n\nr1 = ingest(\"kb/42\", \"Refunds take 5 days. Chat support is 24/7.\", commit=\"a1b2c3\")\ndoc_node = next(ds for r, d, ds in edges if r == r1 and d == \"reads\")\nprint(sorted(forward(doc_node)))      # the chunks to delete if kb/42 must be erased\nprint(backward(\"chunk:kb/42:%s:0\" % doc_node.split(\"@\")[1]))\n```\n\nIn a real system the same records would be OpenLineage events to a catalog, and the serving trace would\nlog the chunk ids it retrieved so a user-visible answer links back to `backward(...)`.","tradeoffs":["Record-level lineage makes deletion and citation exact, but it adds a handful of metadata fields to every chunk and row; at billions of records the storage is real, and table-level may be all the warehouse side needs.","Run-time capture is accurate but requires every job to be instrumented; one uninstrumented job creates a hole in the graph exactly where you later need it, so coverage is worth enforcing in CI.","A full lineage catalog gives impact analysis across teams, but it is another system to run; a single lineage table owned by the AI platform is enough until multiple teams need to query it.","Versioned dataset nodes give precise reproduction but mean immutable snapshots or content hashes for every input, which changes how pipelines write data; mutable tables with no snapshot are the enemy."],"pitfalls":["Recording dataset names instead of dataset versions; the symptom is a graph that says \"the training job read the events table\" and cannot tell you which rows were present.","Documenting lineage in a diagram or README rather than emitting it from jobs; the symptom is a graph that is confidently wrong six weeks later.","Storing chunk metadata without the ingest run id or source version; the symptom is being unable to explain why two retrievals of the \"same\" page differ after a re-index.","Treating the serving trace and the ingestion lineage as separate systems; the symptom is a citation that cannot be linked to a specific document version when a user disputes it.","Skipping lineage for manual one-off fixes (\"I just patched that table by hand\"); the symptom is an unexplained discontinuity nobody can reproduce.","Instrumenting batch pipelines but not request-path writes such as memories, caches and saved answers; the symptom is a deletion drill that finds every chunk yet misses the summaries that quote the document."],"when":"Lineage becomes mandatory the moment a system must honour deletion requests, must explain its outputs,\nor has more than one pipeline feeding retrieval or training, which describes nearly every production AI\nsystem. Start with the minimum: a run table with inputs, outputs, versions and code commit, plus source\nand run identifiers on every chunk. Adopt a catalog when more than one team needs the graph. Measure\ncoverage (fraction of jobs emitting lineage), and test it by running a deletion drill: pick a document,\ntraverse forward, and verify every derived artifact is found. In interviews, \"how would you handle a\nright-to-erasure request across a RAG and fine-tuning stack\" is answered by describing forward traversal\nover a lineage graph; without it the honest answer is \"we could not\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments ingestion and training jobs to emit run events with versions and facets, and stamps every chunk with its source version and ingest run id.","architect":"Decides the lineage granularity per data domain, picks declarative versus run-time capture and the catalog, and makes lineage coverage a release requirement.","fde":"Uses the graph to answer a customer's auditor or DPO: which of their records are in which indexes and datasets, and proves deletion with a forward traversal."},"check":{"q":"Retrieval quality for one product area dropped sharply after last night's pipeline runs. Nothing was\ndeployed. How does lineage help you find the cause, and what would you need to have recorded?","a":"Take a failing query, find the chunks that should have been retrieved, and walk backward: each chunk\nrecords its source document version, parser version, chunker version, embedding version and ingest run.\nCompare those facets against the same chunks from the last good index. If the source version changed, the\nupstream content moved or was reformatted; if parser or chunker versions changed, a dependency upgrade\nslipped in without a deploy of your code; if the embedding version changed, a model revision moved under\nyou; if only the run differs, compare run facets such as row counts and schema fingerprints to find a\npartial or failed load. For this to work, jobs must emit run events with code and container versions and\nper-dataset statistics, and chunks must carry their run and source versions. Without versions, you only\nknow that \"ingestion ran\", which is where the investigation would stall."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must lineage nodes be dataset versions rather than dataset names?","options":["Names are not unique across teams","A job reads a specific state of a dataset, and reproduction or deletion requires knowing which state","Versions are easier to store in a graph database","Names change too often to be reliable identifiers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"\"Job J read table T\" cannot answer which rows were present or let you rebuild the output. Recording the snapshot or content hash as the node makes the graph a recipe rather than a sketch."},{"q":"Which lineage traversal answers \"which artifacts must be purged when this document is deleted\"?","options":["Backward traversal from the index to the document","Forward traversal from the document version to everything derived from it","A search of the vector index for similar content","A diff of the last two ingestion runs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Deletion propagation starts from the source and follows writes downstream: chunks, vectors, caches, eval examples, fine-tuning rows. Backward traversal explains a single artifact; similarity search would miss derived data that no longer resembles the original."},{"q":"Why is lineage captured by jobs at run time preferred over a maintained diagram of the pipeline?","options":["Diagrams cannot represent cycles in the pipeline","Run-time capture is cheaper to implement","A diagram describes the intended graph; run-time events describe what actually ran, with which versions","Regulators require machine-readable formats"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Declared lineage drifts the moment someone changes a job, and it never knows which input version a given run used. Events emitted as a side effect of doing the work are accurate by construction."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/data-contracts","sources":["Official docs: Confluent Schema Registry","Official docs: Great Expectations"],"why":"Imagine the support-ticket pipeline that feeds a RAG index and a fine-tuning set. The ticketing team\nrenames `resolution_text` to `resolution` and starts writing `category` as an integer id instead of a\nlabel. Nothing errors. The chunker reads a missing field as an empty string and emits empty chunks; the\nmetadata filter on `category == \"billing\"` silently matches nothing; the fine-tuning set gets three weeks\nof examples with blank targets. The first signal is a product manager asking why answers got worse, and\nthe investigation takes days because the producer did not know anyone depended on those fields.\n\nTraditional applications fail fast on this: a typed API client breaks at compile time or returns a 400.\nData pipelines do not, *because* data flows through files and tables with no type-checked boundary, and\n*because* consumers are typically discovered after the fact. AI pipelines make it worse: embeddings and\ngradients absorb any input, so bad data becomes a slightly wrong vector or weight, invisible until an\nevaluation runs, if one runs.\n\nThe naive alternative is defensive consumer-side validation: each pipeline checks what it reads. It helps,\nbut it fails on three counts. The check runs after the bad data was written, so the producer still has to\nbe found and persuaded to fix it. Every consumer writes its own checks and they disagree. And semantic\nchanges (the same column now in cents instead of dollars) pass schema checks entirely. A contract makes\nthe producer responsible for a published, versioned specification and enforces it at the point of\nproduction, where the person who made the change sees the failure.","how":"### What a contract contains\n1. **Schema.** Field names, types, nullability, and allowed values, in a machine-readable form (JSON\n   Schema, Avro, Protobuf, or a Pydantic or dataclass model that generates one). This catches renames and\n   type changes.\n2. **Semantics.** What each field means, its unit, its timezone, its reference data. `amount` is \"USD,\n   two decimal places, net of tax\". This is prose plus, where possible, checks: ranges, regexes,\n   cross-field rules such as `closed_at >= opened_at`.\n3. **Quality guarantees.** Null rates, uniqueness of keys, referential integrity, row-count bounds per\n   partition, and distribution expectations that catch \"all categories are suddenly 0\".\n4. **Freshness and delivery.** How often data lands, maximum lag, partitioning, and how late data is\n   handled.\n5. **Ownership and change policy.** Who owns the dataset, how changes are proposed, what counts as a\n   breaking change, and how long a deprecated version stays available.\n6. **Version.** The contract has a version; the data carries it, so consumers know which contract a\n   record was written under.\n7. **Known consumers.** Which pipelines depend on which fields. This is what lets a producer's CI tell the\n   author of a breaking change whom it will break; the list is best derived from lineage (a forward\n   traversal from the dataset) rather than maintained by hand, because hand-kept lists miss the newest\n   consumer, which is usually the AI pipeline.\n\nThe schema part is usually expressed in an existing format; the remaining parts are often kept in a YAML\nspecification beside it. Open specifications for the whole document exist (the Open Data Contract\nStandard is one), but the format matters far less than that it is versioned, reviewed and checked by\nmachines.\n\n### Where enforcement lives\nThe contract is enforced at the **producer boundary**, *because* a failure there is attributed correctly\nand happens before any consumer reads bad data. Concretely: the producing service validates each record\nagainst the schema before publishing; a streaming platform's schema registry rejects incompatible schema\nversions at registration time (Confluent's registry has backward, forward and full compatibility modes\nfor exactly this); a batch pipeline runs a quality suite (Great Expectations or a hand-rolled equivalent)\non the output partition and refuses to mark it complete if checks fail. Records that violate the contract\ngo to a dead-letter location with the violation attached, not silently dropped and not passed through.\n\nPer-record dead-lettering needs a batch-level circuit breaker beside it. If 0.1% of a partition violates\nthe contract, quarantining those rows and publishing the rest is right. If 40% violates it, the cause is\nalmost certainly a systematic upstream change, and publishing the surviving 60% hands consumers a biased\nsample that looks valid: a training set missing every ticket from one product, say. So the contract also\nstates a violation-rate threshold above which the whole partition fails and nothing is published,\n*because* \"no new data today\" is a loud, safe failure and \"a skewed subset\" is a silent one.\n\nConsumers still validate, but against the same published contract, so the two sides cannot disagree about\nwhat valid means. The contract file lives in version control; a CI check diffs proposed changes and\nclassifies them as compatible (adding an optional field) or breaking (removing or renaming a field,\nnarrowing a type, changing a unit), and breaking changes require a new major version and a migration\nwindow during which both versions are produced.\n\n### Compatibility rules\nThe two directions are easy to swap, so use the schema-registry definitions. *Backward* compatible means\na consumer using the new schema can read data written with the old one; it lets you upgrade consumers\nfirst, and permits deleting fields and adding fields that have defaults. *Forward* compatible means data\nwritten with the new schema can be read by consumers still on the old one; it lets producers upgrade\nfirst, and permits adding fields and deleting only fields that had defaults. *Full* compatibility is\nboth. Renaming a field breaks both directions. For AI consumers fed by an independent producer, forward\ncompatibility is the one that matters most day to day, *because* the producer ships first and a chunker or\nfeature pipeline deployed last month must keep working on today's data; backward compatibility matters\nwhen you deploy a new consumer and reprocess history written under older schemas. Write the direction\ninto the contract explicitly, since teams outside the streaming world often use the words the other way\nround.\n\n### Contracts specific to AI pipelines\nThe datasets that feed AI have fields the rest of the company does not think about, and the contract is\nwhere they become explicit:\n- **Text fields intended for embedding:** encoding, language tag, maximum length, and whether the field\n  may contain PII (which decides redaction before embedding).\n- **Metadata used for filtering:** the exact value set for `tenant_id`, `access_scope`, `product`, since a\n  filter on a value that stopped being produced returns nothing with no error.\n- **Labels for fine-tuning or evaluation:** the label taxonomy version and the annotation guideline\n  version, because a change in labeling policy is a semantic break even when the schema is unchanged.\n- **Timestamps for point-in-time joins:** which timestamp is the event time versus the ingestion time,\n  because confusing them creates leakage in training data.\n\n### Testing the contract\nProducer CI runs the validation suite against sample output; consumer CI runs its pipeline against\nfixture data generated from the contract; and a compatibility check compares the proposed contract\nversion against the current one. This is the data equivalent of contract testing between services.","example":"A contract as code for a support-ticket feed: a Pydantic model carrying schema and semantic checks, a\ncontract version embedded in every record, and a producer-side gate that routes violations to a\ndead-letter list instead of publishing them.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom typing import Literal\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field, ValidationError, model_validator\n\nCONTRACT_VERSION = \"support_tickets.v2\"\n\nclass Ticket(BaseModel):\n    contract_version: Literal[\"support_tickets.v2\"] = CONTRACT_VERSION\n    ticket_id: str = Field(pattern=r\"^T-\\d{6,}$\")\n    tenant_id: str = Field(min_length=1)\n    category: Literal[\"billing\", \"login\", \"bug\", \"feature\", \"other\"]   # closed value set\n    opened_at: datetime\n    closed_at: datetime | None = None\n    resolution: str | None = Field(default=None, max_length=8000)       # text that will be embedded\n    language: Literal[\"en\", \"de\", \"fr\"] = \"en\"\n    contains_pii: bool                                                  # decides redaction before embedding\n\n    @model_validator(mode=\"after\")\n    def semantics(self):                    # tz check first: naive-vs-aware compare raises TypeError\n        if any(t is not None and t.tzinfo is None for t in (self.opened_at, self.closed_at)):\n            raise ValueError(\"timestamps must be timezone-aware (UTC)\")\n        if self.closed_at and self.closed_at < self.opened_at:\n            raise ValueError(\"closed_at before opened_at\")\n        if self.closed_at and not self.resolution:\n            raise ValueError(\"closed ticket must carry a resolution\")\n        return self\n\ndef publish(raw_records: list[dict]):\n    good, dead = [], []\n    for r in raw_records:\n        try:\n            good.append(Ticket(**r).model_dump())\n        except ValidationError as e:\n            dead.append({\"record\": r, \"violations\": e.errors(include_url=False)})\n    return good, dead\n\nnow = datetime.now(timezone.utc)\ngood, dead = publish([\n    {\"ticket_id\": \"T-000123\", \"tenant_id\": \"acme\", \"category\": \"billing\",\n     \"opened_at\": now, \"closed_at\": now, \"resolution\": \"Refunded.\", \"contains_pii\": False},\n    {\"ticket_id\": \"T-000124\", \"tenant_id\": \"acme\", \"category\": 3,       # upstream changed to int ids\n     \"opened_at\": now, \"contains_pii\": False},\n])\nprint(len(good), \"published;\", len(dead), \"dead-lettered:\", dead[0][\"violations\"][0][\"msg\"])\n```\n\nThe second record is the silent failure from the \"Why it exists\" section, now caught at the producer with\na message naming the field, before the embedding job ever sees it.","tradeoffs":["Producer-side enforcement attributes failures correctly and stops bad data early, but it asks the producing team to own a specification; without organisational buy-in, consumer-side checks are the realistic fallback.","A closed value set for categorical fields makes filters safe, but every new business category needs a contract change; if categories churn weekly, use an open set plus a reference table the contract points to.","Strict schemas reject records with unexpected fields and prevent drift, but they also reject harmless additions; \"ignore unknown fields, forbid missing ones\" is the usual compromise.","Dead-lettering violations preserves data for repair, but a dead-letter queue nobody monitors is a silent data loss; alert on its rate and age.","Versioning with dual-production windows makes breaking changes safe, but doubles the producer's output for the window; for high-volume streams, make the window short and the migration automated."],"pitfalls":["Validating only schema and not semantics; the symptom is a model whose quality drops with no schema errors, traced weeks later to a unit or label-taxonomy change.","Enforcing the contract only in the consumer; the symptom is the AI team discovering every upstream change and arguing for a fix after the damage is done.","Omitting the contract version from the records themselves; the symptom is a reprocessing job that cannot tell which rules applied to which historical rows.","Treating filter metadata as free text; the symptom is `tenant_id` values with different casing or whitespace making ACL filters leak or return nothing.","Silently dropping violating records to keep the pipeline green; the symptom is row counts drifting down and a training set that over-represents whatever happened to validate.","Making every field required to be safe; the symptom is the producer defaulting fields to placeholder values just to pass, which is worse than null.","Dead-lettering per record with no batch-level threshold; the symptom is a partition that publishes on schedule with a fraction of its usual rows and a quietly shifted category distribution."],"when":"Introduce a contract for any dataset that crosses a team boundary into an embedding, fine-tuning, feature\nor evaluation pipeline; these are the consumers that fail silently, so they benefit most. A dataset\nproduced and consumed by the same team can get by with in-pipeline validation until a second consumer\nappears. Measure the dead-letter rate (and alert on its change, not only its level), partitions failed by the\nbatch threshold, the time from an upstream change to its detection (should be minutes, at the producer),\nand the number of breaking changes per quarter. In day-to-day work the contract\nis the file you open before adding a metadata filter or a training feature. In interviews, \"how do you\nkeep upstream schema changes from degrading your model\" is answered with producer-side contracts,\ncompatibility checks in CI and versioned records, not with \"we validate inputs\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the contract as a typed model, wires validation and dead-lettering into the producer, and generates fixtures from the contract for consumer tests.","architect":"Decides which datasets require contracts, the compatibility policy and versioning scheme, and the organisational rule that producers own their published data.","fde":"Negotiates the contract with a customer's data owners during onboarding, including PII flags and tenant scoping, so the pipeline is protected from their internal changes."},"check":{"q":"A customer's CRM team wants to change the `region` field from free text to a code list and add a\n`segment` field next month. Your RAG metadata filters and a fine-tuned router depend on `region`. How do you\nhandle the change?","a":"Classify the two changes. Adding `segment` as an optional field with a default is compatible in both\ndirections and can ship under the current contract version once documented. Changing `region` from free text to codes is a\nbreaking semantic change even though the type stays string: existing filters compare against the old\nvalues and the router was trained on them. So it requires a new major contract version. Agree a migration\nwindow in which the producer emits both `region` (old values) and `region_code` (new), with the version\nstamped on each record. During the window, update the filter logic to use codes, rebuild the index\nmetadata, and retrain or remap the router with the new values, validating on the eval set. Run the\ncompatibility check in CI so the old field cannot be dropped before consumers have migrated, and dead-letter\nany record whose `region_code` is outside the agreed list."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are AI pipelines more exposed to upstream data changes than typical application code?","options":["They process more rows per second than applications","Embeddings and training absorb malformed inputs without errors, so damage appears only as degraded quality","They usually run in a different programming language from the producer","Their schemas change more frequently"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A typed application fails fast on a renamed field. An embedding job turns an empty string into a vector and a training job fits whatever labels it is given, so the only symptom is a worse index or model, discovered late."},{"q":"Why is the contract enforced at the producer rather than only in consumers?","options":["Producers have more compute available for validation","Consumers cannot parse schema files","A failure at the producer is attributed to the change that caused it and happens before any bad data is read","It is required by schema registries"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Producer-side enforcement makes the person who changed the data see the failure immediately. Consumer checks still matter, but they run after the bad data exists and leave the AI team to track down the cause."},{"q":"Which change passes a schema check yet should be treated as a breaking contract change?","options":["Adding a new optional field with a default value","Widening a string field's maximum length","Switching an amount column from dollars to cents while keeping its type","Adding a new enum value to a category list that consumers treat as open"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The type is unchanged, so schema validation passes, but every consumer's interpretation is now wrong by a factor of 100. Semantic checks (ranges, units documented and tested) and a version bump are what catch and manage this class of change."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","sources":["Mitchell et al., 2019 — Model Cards for Model Reporting","Gebru et al., 2021 — Datasheets for Datasets","Official docs: Hugging Face Hub"],"why":"A fine-tuned model lands in the registry as `router-v4.safetensors`. Six months later the questions\nstart. Legal asks whether any training data came from a source with a non-commercial licence. A customer\nasks whether their data was used. The eval team notices suspiciously high scores and wants to know whether\nthe benchmark leaked into training. Security asks whether the base model was downloaded from the official\nrepository or a lookalike. An engineer wants to reproduce the run to fix a regression. Every one of these\nis answerable only if, at build time, someone recorded the inputs precisely and made the record\ntamper-evident.\n\nThe naive alternative is the model card written after the fact and the README in the dataset folder.\nThey fail because they are prose written from memory: they describe what the author believes the inputs\nwere, not what the job actually read. A dataset folder that was \"the cleaned support tickets\" when the\nREADME was written has since had rows added and removed. A base model pulled by name resolves to a\ndifferent revision today. Prose cannot be diffed, verified or traced.\n\nThe second naive alternative is \"git is enough\": commit the training script and call it reproducible.\nCode is one of six inputs. The data snapshot, the base model revision, the environment (library and\nCUDA versions), the hyperparameters and the random seed all change outputs, and none of them lives in\nthe training script. Provenance is the discipline of capturing all of them, as identifiers that can be\nchecked, at the moment the artifact is created.","how":"### Content addressing: identity by hash\nThe foundation is that every input and output is identified by a cryptographic hash of its bytes, not by\na name or path. A dataset snapshot is the sorted list of its files with their SHA-256 digests, hashed\nagain into one dataset digest (a Merkle-style root). A model artifact is its file digests. This is chosen\n*because* a hash is the only identifier that is stable across copies, cannot drift when someone edits the\nfolder, and lets a reader verify they hold the same bytes. Hugging Face model repositories are git\nrepositories, so a commit hash identifies a revision exactly; always pin `revision` rather than relying\non the default branch, which moves.\n\n### The build manifest\nEvery training or fine-tuning run writes a manifest beside its output, containing:\n- base model: repository id and revision hash, plus file digests;\n- datasets: each snapshot digest, row counts, and the filter or sampling parameters used;\n- code: commit hash and container or environment digest, with library versions;\n- parameters: the full hyperparameter set and the seed;\n- outputs: checkpoint digests and the eval results with the eval set's own digest;\n- actor and time: who or what launched the run, when.\n\nMerged, distilled and adapter models list every parent artifact as an input (each with its own manifest\ndigest), *because* a merge has several ancestors and the licence and contamination questions must be\nanswered for all of them. The format of the record matters less than its completeness; supply-chain\nattestation formats from software (in-toto and SLSA-style provenance) and bill-of-materials formats with\nML profiles (CycloneDX and SPDX both have them) are being adopted so registries and scanners can read\nmanifests without bespoke parsers.\n\nThe manifest is written by the job, not by a person, *because* that is the only way it describes what\nran. It is stored in the model registry as the artifact's metadata and should also be committed where\nthe lineage system can link it to dataset versions.\n\n### Dataset documentation\nDatasheets (Gebru et al.) formalize the questions a dataset should answer: motivation, composition,\ncollection process, preprocessing, intended uses, distribution and maintenance. For an AI team the\noperationally important subset is: source systems and their terms, consent or licence basis, PII handling\napplied, the filtering recipe and its parameters, known gaps, and the eval sets that must never overlap\nwith it. Attach this as a structured file next to the snapshot digest, so documentation and bytes are\nversioned together.\n\n### Model documentation\nModel cards (Mitchell et al.) do the same for models: intended use, out-of-scope use, training data\nsummary, evaluation results broken down by relevant slices, limitations and ethical considerations. The\nprovenance manifest is the machine-readable backbone; the model card is the human-readable layer that\nreferences it. Regulation increasingly expects both: the EU AI Act, for example, requires technical\ndocumentation covering data and evaluation for high-risk systems, and for general-purpose model providers\ntechnical documentation plus a public summary of training content. Templates, codes of practice and\napplication dates are still being phased in, so check current obligations rather than relying on a\nsummary.\n\n### Synthetic and model-generated data\nA growing share of fine-tuning data is generated by another model: distilled answers, synthetic\ninstructions, LLM-written labels. Such a dataset's inputs are not just its seed data but also the\ngenerator model and revision, the prompt template version, the sampling parameters and the filter that\nkept or discarded outputs. Record all of them, *because* the generator's behaviour is now part of your\nmodel (its errors and biases propagate), a change of generator explains a change of downstream quality,\nand the generator's usage terms travel with its outputs: some providers' terms restrict using outputs to\ntrain competing models, which is a question for counsel that the manifest must make answerable.\n\n### Licence and terms tracking\nEach dataset and base model carries its licence identifier in the manifest. The safe engineering policy\nis that a derived artifact carries the combined obligations of all its inputs, computed by walking the\nlineage graph, *because* publishing a fine-tune under a permissive licence does not release it from the\nbase model's terms (many model licences explicitly bind derivatives). Licences are not a simple\nrestrictiveness ranking: two inputs can impose different, even incompatible, obligations, and whether a\ngiven dataset licence reaches the trained weights is a legal question, so the graph's job is to surface\nevery applicable term for counsel, not to decide it.\nThis is also where \"the customer's data may not be used to train shared models\" is enforced: the\nmanifest of any shared model must show no customer-restricted dataset digests among its inputs.\n\n### Integrity and supply chain\nA hash tells you what you have; a signature tells you who produced it. Signing model artifacts and\nmanifests (Sigstore-style keyless signing is one approach being adopted for ML artifacts) lets a deployer\nverify that a checkpoint came from the registry's build pipeline, not from a laptop or a tampered\ndownload. Prefer `safetensors` over pickle-based formats, because loading a pickle executes arbitrary\ncode embedded in the file; a signed safetensors file is both authentic and inert. Record dependency\ndigests too, since a training run is only as trustworthy as the libraries that ran it.\n\n### Using provenance\n- **Contamination check:** hash the eval set's examples and the training snapshot's examples; exact\n  overlap is found by digest comparison without re-reading the pipeline. Hashes only catch exact copies,\n  so normalize text before hashing and add n-gram or embedding-similarity checks for reformatted or\n  paraphrased leaks.\n- **Data subject requests:** the dataset snapshot digests in a model's manifest, joined with lineage,\n  answer whether a person's records contributed to a model.\n- **Reproduction:** the manifest is the command line: same base revision, same snapshot, same commit,\n  same parameters and seed. Bitwise identity is not guaranteed on GPUs, but the result should land within\n  the known run-to-run variance.\n- **Incident response:** when a model misbehaves, compare its manifest with the last good one; the diff\n  is the list of suspects.","example":"A manifest writer for a fine-tuning run. It hashes the dataset snapshot and base model files, captures\ncode and environment identifiers, and writes a JSON manifest beside the checkpoint. The training call is a\nstub; everything around it is what provenance requires.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, subprocess, sys, platform, time\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef file_digest(p: Path) -> str:\n    h = hashlib.sha256()\n    with p.open(\"rb\") as f:\n        for block in iter(lambda: f.read(1 << 20), b\"\"):\n            h.update(block)\n    return h.hexdigest()\n\ndef tree_digest(root: Path) -> dict:\n    files = sorted(p for p in root.rglob(\"*\") if p.is_file())\n    per_file = {str(p.relative_to(root)): file_digest(p) for p in files}\n    root_hash = hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(per_file, sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest()\n    return {\"root\": root_hash, \"files\": len(per_file), \"per_file\": per_file}\n\ndef git_commit() -> str:\n    return subprocess.check_output([\"git\", \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\"], text=True).strip()\n\ndef train(out_dir: Path, params: dict) -> Path:       # stand-in for the real trainer\n    out_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    ckpt = out_dir / \"model.safetensors\"\n    ckpt.write_bytes(b\"weights-placeholder\")\n    return ckpt\n\ndef run(base_model: dict, dataset_dir: Path, eval_dir: Path, out_dir: Path, params: dict):\n    data, evalset = tree_digest(dataset_dir), tree_digest(eval_dir)\n    overlap = set(data[\"per_file\"].values()) & set(evalset[\"per_file\"].values())\n    if overlap:\n        raise SystemExit(f\"eval files present in training snapshot: {len(overlap)}\")\n    ckpt = train(out_dir, params)\n    manifest = {\n        \"base_model\": base_model,                       # {\"repo\": ..., \"revision\": <commit hash>}\n        \"datasets\": [{\"path\": str(dataset_dir), \"digest\": data[\"root\"], \"files\": data[\"files\"],\n                      \"license\": \"customer-restricted:acme\"}],\n        \"eval_set_digest\": evalset[\"root\"],\n        \"code\": {\"commit\": git_commit(), \"python\": sys.version.split()[0], \"platform\": platform.platform()},\n        \"params\": params,\n        \"outputs\": {\"checkpoint\": str(ckpt), \"digest\": file_digest(ckpt)},\n        \"created_at\": time.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ\", time.gmtime()),\n    }\n    (out_dir / \"manifest.json\").write_text(json.dumps(manifest, indent=2, sort_keys=True))\n    return manifest\n```\n\nThe overlap check is deliberately crude (whole-file hashes); a real contamination check hashes individual\nexamples or n-grams. The licence field on the dataset is what later blocks this checkpoint from being\npromoted to a shared, multi-customer deployment.","tradeoffs":["Content-addressed snapshots make inputs exact but require immutable copies or a lakehouse with snapshot isolation; hashing a mutable table \"as of now\" is cheap and nearly useless.","Full per-file digests for large corpora cost a scan per run; for multi-terabyte datasets, hash partition manifests the lakehouse already maintains rather than every byte again.","Signing artifacts gives tamper evidence but adds key management and a verification step at load time; skip it only for throwaway experiments that never reach a registry.","Rich datasheets improve downstream decisions but are written by people and go stale; keep the machine-written manifest authoritative and the prose as commentary on it.","Pinning base model revisions protects reproducibility but means security patches to a model repo are not picked up automatically; make upgrading the revision an explicit, evaluated change."],"pitfalls":["Referencing the base model by name without a revision; the symptom is a reproduction attempt that silently uses a newer upstream commit and lands outside the expected variance.","Writing the model card from memory after the run; the symptom is documented training data that does not match the snapshot the job actually read.","Hashing a dataset directory that was later edited in place; the symptom is a manifest digest that matches nothing on disk when an audit asks for the bytes.","Loading pickle-format checkpoints from an unverified source; the symptom is arbitrary code execution at load time, usually discovered by a security review rather than by the model misbehaving.","Forgetting licence propagation through fine-tunes and merges; the symptom is a commercial deployment of a model whose ancestor forbids it.","Treating eval results as part of the model card but not recording the eval set digest; the symptom is scores that cannot be compared across runs because the set quietly changed.","Recording a synthetic dataset's digest but not the generator model and prompt version that produced it; the symptom is a quality shift after regenerating \"the same\" dataset that nobody can explain."],"when":"Record provenance for every artifact that reaches a registry: every fine-tune, every merged model, every\ndataset snapshot used for training or evaluation, and every eval set. Experiments that never leave a\nnotebook can skip signing but should still capture the manifest, since the one that works will be\npromoted. Measure coverage (fraction of registry artifacts with complete manifests), reproduction success\nrate on a sampled run, and time to answer \"what was this trained on\". In day-to-day work provenance shows\nup as the manifest you read before deploying someone else's checkpoint. In interviews, \"how would you\nprove your model was not trained on the benchmark\" is answered with dataset and eval digests and an\noverlap check, not with \"we were careful\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Makes the training job write the manifest, pins base model revisions and dependency digests, and adds the contamination and licence checks as pipeline gates.","architect":"Defines what a complete manifest contains, requires it for registry promotion, and sets the licence inheritance and customer-data isolation rules the manifests enforce.","fde":"Shows a customer's compliance team the manifest proving which of their datasets touched which models, and that none reached a shared model."},"check":{"q":"A customer alleges that a shared model you deploy to several clients was trained on their\nproprietary documents. How do you establish the truth, and what must have been in place beforehand?","a":"Pull the deployed model's manifest from the registry and walk its ancestry: the fine-tune's dataset\nsnapshot digests, its base model revision, and recursively the manifests of any merged or distilled\ninputs. Each dataset snapshot carries a licence or restriction tag and a lineage link to its source\nsystems. If no snapshot in the closure carries the customer's restriction tag or derives from their source\nsystems, the manifests are strong evidence for the negative across everything your own pipelines fed in\n(a third-party base model's pretraining data is covered only by its vendor's documentation); if one does, you know exactly which run introduced it and which\nother models share that ancestor. For this to be credible, the manifests must have been written by the\njobs at build time with content hashes, signed so they cannot be edited afterwards, and stored with the\nartifact so the deployed digest can be matched to its manifest. Without that, the honest answer is \"we\nbelieve not\", which does not survive a dispute."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is a dataset identified by a content hash rather than by its path or name?","options":["Hashes are shorter than paths","A hash is stable across copies and changes when any byte changes, so it can be verified and cannot drift","Paths are not allowed in model registries","Hashing is required by safetensors"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A name points at whatever is currently in that location, which changes silently. A digest identifies the exact bytes, so a manifest naming it can be checked later and a reader can confirm they hold the same data."},{"q":"Which input is most often missing from \"we committed the training script, so it is reproducible\"?","options":["The programming language version","The name of the engineer who ran it","The exact data snapshot and base model revision the run consumed","The number of GPUs used"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Code is one input among several. The data snapshot and base model revision usually change the output more than anything in the script, and neither is captured by a commit unless the manifest records their digests."},{"q":"Why prefer safetensors over pickle-based checkpoint formats when loading third-party models?","options":["Safetensors files are smaller","Pickle cannot store floating-point tensors","Loading a pickle executes code embedded in the file, while safetensors is a pure data format","Safetensors includes the model card automatically"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Pickle deserialization runs arbitrary Python, so an unverified checkpoint is an executable. A data-only format combined with a verified digest or signature gives you an artifact that is both authentic and inert to load."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","sources":["Regulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR), Article 17 — Right to erasure","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications"],"why":"Privacy law (GDPR's right to erasure, similar rights under CCPA and other regimes) and most enterprise\ncontracts require that personal or customer data is kept no longer than needed and deleted on request\nwithin a bounded time (under GDPR, a response is due without undue delay and within one month, extendable\nby two further months for complex requests). For a classic web application the data lives in a database with backups, and\ndeletion is a `DELETE` plus a backup expiry policy. An AI application breaks that model.\n\nTrace the life of one support conversation. The user's message, containing their name and order number,\nis written to the application database; copied into a prompt; sent to a model vendor whose API logs it;\nrecorded by the observability stack as a trace with full prompt and completion; embedded and stored as a\nmemory vector for future personalization; cached as a semantic-cache entry keyed on its embedding;\nsampled into a weekly eval set; exported into a fine-tuning dataset; and, after the fine-tune, partially\nencoded in model weights. Setting aside the transient prompt, that is eight persisted copies in eight\nsystems, typically owned by several different teams, with at least one (the vendor's logs) outside the\ncompany, and two if the observability stack is a hosted service.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"delete from the primary database and let the rest expire\", fails because several\nof those copies have no expiry (the vector index, the fine-tuning dataset, the weights) and because the\nones that do often expire too late to satisfy a request deadline. The second naive alternative, \"never\nstore anything\", fails because observability, evaluation and improvement all depend on retaining\ninteractions. The architecture has to make retention a per-copy decision and make deletion a traversal\nthat reaches every copy.","how":"### Inventory and classify every copy\nStart with a map of where data lands. For each store, record what classes of data it holds (user content,\nderived content such as embeddings and summaries, operational metadata, aggregate metrics), whether it\nis keyed by a subject identifier, and who owns it. The lineage system supplies most of this map for\nderived data; the rest (vendor logs, caches, backups) is documented by hand. Classification matters\n*because* retention is set per class: an aggregate latency metric can live for years, a raw prompt with a\ncustomer's address should not.\n\n### Retention classes and TTLs\nAssign each store a retention policy: a time-to-live after which data is deleted automatically, or\n\"retain while the relationship exists\". Derived data inherits the shortest TTL of its inputs *because*\nan embedding of a deleted message is still a representation of it. Enforce TTLs mechanically: database\npartitions dropped by date, object storage lifecycle rules, vector collections partitioned by ingestion\nmonth, trace stores with built-in retention. Policy that depends on someone remembering to run a cleanup\nis not a policy.\n\n### Retention minimums and legal holds\nRetention is not only a maximum. Some data must be kept for a minimum period (financial records, security\naudit logs, records a regulator requires), and litigation can place a *legal hold* on data that would\notherwise expire or be erased. GDPR's erasure right itself has exceptions, including data needed to comply\nwith a legal obligation or to establish or defend legal claims. So each retention class needs both a\nfloor and a ceiling, and the deletion job must consult a hold register before deleting: a held record is\nskipped and reported as held, not deleted and not silently ignored. Resolving conflicts between the\nfloor, the ceiling, a hold and an erasure request is a policy decision for counsel; the architecture's\njob is to make the rule explicit and executable rather than to decide it.\n\nVendors are a store too. Model API providers typically retain request logs for a period for abuse\nmonitoring unless a zero-retention arrangement is in place; the exact terms vary by provider and\ncontract, so the architecture must record what the agreement is and whether it covers the data classes\nyou send.\n\n### Deletion as a propagated job\nA deletion request (one subject, one tenant, or one document) becomes a job that:\n1. Resolves the subject to identifiers in every store, using lineage to find derived artifacts: the\n   document's chunks, their vectors, cache entries that embedded them, eval examples sampled from them,\n   fine-tuning rows that include them.\n2. Issues deletes to each store, idempotently, with retries, and records a per-store receipt.\n3. Verifies by re-querying each store for the identifiers.\n4. Writes a tombstone so re-ingestion from a still-live source does not resurrect the data, and so a\n   later audit can show when and where deletion happened.\n\nThe job is idempotent and resumable *because* it fans out across many systems and some will be down.\nPartial completion must be visible, not silently treated as done.\n\n### Store-specific mechanics\n- **Vector indexes:** deletion must be keyed by metadata (`subject_id`, `document_id`), so those fields\n  must exist on every vector. Many ANN implementations implement deletion as a soft-delete marker until\n  compaction, segment merge or rebuild, which means the vector may remain on disk after it stops being returned; treat\n  the index as a derived store that can be rebuilt from the source of truth, and schedule rebuilds.\n- **Caches:** semantic and exact-match response caches hold prompts and completions; key them so that\n  entries carrying a subject's data can be found, or give them a TTL short enough that deletion\n  deadlines are met by expiry alone.\n- **Traces and logs:** either redact PII before the trace is written, so the trace store holds nothing\n  deletable, or keep traces keyed by subject with a retention short enough to expire within the deadline.\n  Redaction at write time is the more robust choice.\n- **Backups and immutable stores:** you cannot delete one row from a backup. Two patterns are common.\n  The simpler is a short backup rotation plus a deletion log: backups age out within a defined window,\n  and any restore replays the recorded deletions before the data goes live again. The stronger is\n  crypto-shredding: encrypt each subject's (or tenant's) data under its own key, and delete the key. The\n  ciphertext remains but is unrecoverable, which is widely treated as meeting the intent of erasure for\n  backups and append-only logs; regulators have not uniformly codified that view, so confirm the\n  approach with your privacy counsel.\n- **Summaries and memories:** an LLM-written summary or agent memory derived from a conversation is a new\n  document that may blend several people's data. It cannot be filtered row by row; it must record which\n  conversations and subjects it was derived from, and erasing one subject means deleting the summary or\n  regenerating it from the remaining inputs.\n- **Training datasets:** rows must carry subject or tenant identifiers so they can be removed, and the\n  dataset's snapshot digest changes, which flows into provenance.\n- **Model weights:** data that has been trained into a model cannot be deleted from it; machine\n  unlearning techniques exist but are not reliable enough to certify. The architectural answers are to\n  keep deletable data out of training (redact, or train only on data with consent terms that survive\n  deletion requests), to time-box models so a retrain on the cleaned dataset happens within the deadline,\n  or to accept retraining as the deletion mechanism and budget for it.\n\n### Tenant-level deletion\nEnterprise offboarding deletes everything for a tenant. Partitioning every store by tenant (separate\ncollections, prefixes or keys) turns this from a filtered scan into dropping a partition, which is both\nfaster and more verifiable. Per-tenant encryption keys make the backup problem disappear for the same\nreason as crypto-shredding.\n\n### Proof\nThe output of a deletion job is a report: request id, a keyed reference to the subject, each store\ntouched, receipts, verification results, and the tombstone id. Regulators and customers ask for this, and the job is the only thing that\ncan produce it accurately.","example":"A deletion orchestrator that fans out over registered stores, retries, verifies, and produces a receipt.\nStores are small stand-ins; the shape of the job is what matters.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, hmac, time\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\nTOMB_KEY = b\"from-a-secret-store\"                       # tombstones must not hold the raw id\ndef tomb_id(subject): return hmac.new(TOMB_KEY, subject.encode(), hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()\n\n@dataclass\nclass Store:\n    name: str\n    rows: dict = field(default_factory=dict)           # id -> {\"subject\": ..., ...}\n    flaky: int = 0                                     # simulate transient failures\n    def find(self, subject):\n        return [i for i, r in self.rows.items() if r[\"subject\"] == subject]\n    def delete(self, ids):\n        if self.flaky: self.flaky -= 1; raise ConnectionError(self.name)\n        for i in ids: self.rows.pop(i, None)\n\nclass KeyVault(Store):                                  # crypto-shredding for backups\n    def find(self, subject): return [subject] if subject in self.rows else []\n\ndef erase(subject: str, stores: list[Store], tombstones: set, max_tries=3) -> dict:\n    receipt = {\"subject_ref\": tomb_id(subject)[:16], \"stores\": {}, \"complete\": True}\n    for s in stores:\n        ids = s.find(subject)\n        for attempt in range(1, max_tries + 1):\n            try:\n                s.delete(ids); break\n            except ConnectionError:\n                time.sleep(0)                           # backoff in real code\n        remaining = s.find(subject)\n        ok = not remaining\n        receipt[\"stores\"][s.name] = {\"found\": len(ids), \"verified\": ok, \"attempts\": attempt}\n        receipt[\"complete\"] &= ok\n    tombstones.add(tomb_id(subject))                    # blocks re-ingestion from live sources\n    receipt[\"tombstone\"] = tomb_id(subject) in tombstones\n    return receipt\n\napp_db   = Store(\"app_db\",   {\"m1\": {\"subject\": \"u42\", \"text\": \"order 991\"}, \"m2\": {\"subject\": \"u7\"}})\nvectors  = Store(\"vectors\",  {\"c1\": {\"subject\": \"u42\", \"doc\": \"m1\"}}, flaky=1)\ncache    = Store(\"sem_cache\",{\"k9\": {\"subject\": \"u42\"}})\nbackups  = KeyVault(\"backup_keys\", {\"u42\": {\"subject\": \"u42\", \"key\": \"...\"}})\ntomb = set()\nprint(erase(\"u42\", [app_db, vectors, cache, backups], tomb))\n# ingestion computes tomb_id(subject) and skips any record whose id is in `tomb`\n```\n\nThe `complete` flag is what an audit reads; a `False` means the job must be rerun or escalated, never\nquietly closed. In production the store list comes from the inventory, and the derived-artifact ids come\nfrom a forward lineage traversal rather than a per-store scan.","tradeoffs":["Short TTLs on traces and caches make deletion deadlines trivial to meet but shrink the window for debugging and evaluation; the balance shifts toward longer retention once PII is redacted at write time.","Crypto-shredding handles backups and immutable logs cleanly, but per-subject keys add a key-management system and a lookup on every read; per-tenant keys are far cheaper and cover offboarding, but cannot shred one person inside a tenant, so per-subject erasure then falls back to backup expiry plus replay of the deletion log on restore.","Keeping personal data out of fine-tuning avoids the unlearning problem entirely, but some use cases need it; then retraining on a schedule is the cost of being able to honour deletions.","Partitioning every store by tenant makes offboarding a partition drop, but multiplies collections and indexes; for thousands of small tenants, logical partitioning with tenant keys plus filtered deletes is more practical.","Verification by re-query adds load and time to each deletion job, but a deletion without verification is an assertion, not evidence; skip verification only for stores with transactional guarantees."],"pitfalls":["Vectors stored without subject or document identifiers; the symptom is a deletion request that requires a full index rebuild because nothing can be targeted.","Treating \"the source document was deleted\" as \"the chunks are gone\"; the symptom is retrieval still citing a page that no longer exists.","Relying on ANN soft deletes; the symptom is an audit finding the bytes still on disk months after a confirmed deletion.","Full prompts in traces with multi-year retention; the symptom is an observability stack that becomes the largest PII store in the company and the hardest to delete from.","Re-ingestion resurrecting deleted data because the source system still has it; the symptom is a user deleted last month reappearing in the index after the next crawl.","Forgetting the vendor; the symptom is a data-processing audit asking for the provider's retention terms and nobody knowing whether zero-retention was ever enabled.","Deletion jobs that report success on partial completion; the symptom is a later discovery in a store that was down during the run.","Tombstones and deletion receipts that store the raw email or user id; the symptom is a deny-list that is itself a growing register of exactly the people who asked to be forgotten. Store a keyed hash instead.","Deleting data that is under legal hold because the erasure job never consults the hold register; the symptom surfaces in litigation, which is the worst place to discover it."],"when":"This architecture is required wherever personal data, customer-confidential data or regulated data\nenters prompts, indexes or training sets, which is most enterprise deployments and any consumer product.\nDesign it before the first index exists, because adding subject identifiers to vectors and tenant\npartitioning to collections after the fact is a full rebuild. Measure: time from deletion request to\nverified completion across all stores, number of stores with no automated TTL, and percentage of derived\nartifacts reachable through lineage. Run deletion drills quarterly. In interviews, \"a user invokes their\nright to erasure; walk me through what happens in your RAG system\" should produce the store inventory,\nthe propagated job with receipts, the tombstone, and an honest statement about model weights.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Adds subject and tenant identifiers to every vector, cache entry and training row, builds the idempotent deletion job with receipts, and wires tombstone checks into ingestion.","architect":"Owns the store inventory and retention classes, chooses crypto-shredding and partitioning strategies, and decides whether personal data may enter training at all.","fde":"Maps a customer's data-protection requirements onto the stack, confirms the vendor's retention terms match their contract, and runs a deletion drill with their privacy officer before go-live."},"check":{"q":"Your assistant fine-tunes a model monthly on sampled conversations, and a user invokes their right to\nerasure. The conversations are already in last month's checkpoint. What do you do, and how should the\nsystem have been designed?","a":"Immediately run the propagated deletion for all deletable copies: application records, traces,\nvector memories, caches, the eval and fine-tuning datasets (producing a new dataset snapshot digest), and\nrecord a tombstone and receipt. For the checkpoint, be honest: the data cannot be surgically removed from\nweights with certifiable reliability. The options are to retire the checkpoint and retrain on the cleaned\nsnapshot within the deadline, which the monthly cadence makes feasible if the retrain can be triggered on\ndemand, or to show that the training data was redacted so no personal data was ever in the weights. The\nsystem should have been designed to make one of those true by construction: redact PII before sampling\ninto training data, carry subject identifiers on every training row, keep models time-boxed with an\non-demand retrain path, and document in the vendor agreement what the provider retains. Deletion proof is\nthe job's receipt plus the provenance manifest of the replacement model showing the cleaned snapshot."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is \"delete from the primary database and let everything else expire\" insufficient for an LLM application?","options":["Primary databases cannot delete rows that are referenced by foreign keys","Several derived copies, such as vectors, fine-tuning data and weights, have no expiry at all","Expiry policies are not permitted under privacy regulation","The primary database is usually not the system of record"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Vector indexes, training datasets and model weights persist indefinitely unless a deletion mechanism is designed for them. Expiry works only for stores that have a TTL, and even those may expire after the request deadline."},{"q":"How does crypto-shredding make deletion possible in backups and append-only logs?","options":["It compresses the backup so the deleted rows are overwritten","It rewrites the backup without the subject's rows","Data is encrypted per subject or tenant, and deleting the key makes the remaining ciphertext unrecoverable","It moves the subject's rows to a separate archive with shorter retention"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Immutable stores cannot have individual rows removed. Encrypting under a per-subject key and destroying the key leaves only unreadable ciphertext without modifying the store, which is why it is the common pattern for backups. Compression or a separate archive still leaves the data readable."},{"q":"In the propagated deletion job, what is the tombstone written in the final step for?","options":["To mark the subject's rows as pending so a nightly job can remove them later","To prevent re-ingestion from resurrecting deleted data and to record that deletion occurred","To hold the subject's data for the appeal period before final deletion","To signal the model vendor to purge their logs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Source systems or crawls may still contain the data; the tombstone tells ingestion to skip it and gives auditors a record of what was deleted and when. It is a deny-list, not a holding area."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/feature-stores","sources":["Official docs: Feast"],"why":"A fraud model needs \"number of transactions by this card in the last hour\" and \"customer's average order\nvalue over 90 days\". Those features are computed in two places: a batch SQL job that builds the training\nset from the warehouse, and a service that computes them at request time from a cache or production\ndatabase. The two implementations drift. The batch job counts refunds; the service does not. The batch job\nuses UTC day boundaries; the service uses local time. The model learns from one definition and is scored\nwith another, and accuracy in production is lower than in evaluation for reasons nobody can see. This is\n**training/serving skew**, and it is the first problem a feature store removes by making the definition\nsingular.\n\nThe second problem is subtler. When you build a training set by joining labels to features, the join\nmust use the feature values *as they were at the moment each label's event happened*. Join to today's\nvalues and the model learns from the future: the \"90-day average\" for a transaction last March includes\norders from April. Offline metrics look excellent, production is worse, and the gap is **time leakage**.\nDoing this join correctly by hand across dozens of features with different update cadences is where most\nhand-rolled pipelines go wrong.\n\nThe naive alternative is a shared feature library plus a cache. It fixes definition drift but not\nleakage, does not store history, and gives no answer to \"which feature version did model v12 train on\".\nThe feature store adds a historical store, point-in-time joins, versioned definitions and an online\nserving path, which is the minimum set that solves both problems.","how":"### Entities, features and feature views\nA feature is a named, typed value attached to an **entity** (a customer id, a card id, a merchant id)\nwith an **event timestamp** saying when the value became true. Features are grouped into **feature\nviews** (Feast's term; other systems say feature groups or tables): a set of features for one entity\ncomputed by one transformation from one source, with a TTL that says how long a value stays valid. The\ndefinition lives in code under version control, *because* it must be the single source of truth for\nboth the batch and the serving path.\n\n### The offline store\nHistorical feature values, keyed by entity and event timestamp, in the warehouse or lakehouse. It is\nappend-only by design: a new value for a customer is a new row with a later timestamp, not an overwrite,\n*because* training needs every past value to reconstruct what was known at any given moment.\n\n### The point-in-time join\nGiven a label table with `(entity_id, label_timestamp, label)`, the training set is built by, for each\nrow, selecting from each feature view the latest value with `event_timestamp <= label_timestamp` and\n`label_timestamp - event_timestamp <= ttl`. If no value qualifies, the feature is null, which is the\nhonest answer: at that time the model would not have known it. This \"as-of\" join is the core algorithm\nand is the reason the offline store keeps history. Feast exposes it as `get_historical_features`.\n\n### The boundary must match what serving sees\nThe `<=` in the join is a decision, not a detail. Suppose the feature is \"transactions by this card in\nthe last hour\" and the label is attached to a transaction at 14:00:00. If the feature pipeline has\nalready counted that transaction in a value stamped 14:00:00, an inclusive join hands the model a count\nthat includes the very event being scored. At serving time the scorer runs *before* the transaction is\nrecorded, so it never sees itself in the count. The training and serving views then differ by exactly\none, systematically, which is leakage that looks like a rounding error. Choose strict or inclusive\ncomparison, and any allowance for ingestion delay, to reproduce what the online path can know at\ndecision time.\n\n### Backfills\nA new feature is useless for training until it has history. A *backfill* runs the feature's\ntransformation over historical source data to populate the offline store as if the feature had always\nexisted, which is possible only if the sources are themselves historical (an event log or a snapshot\ntable). If the feature reads a mutable production table that keeps only current state, history cannot be\nreconstructed; the only honest options are to start logging the feature now and wait until enough\nlabelled history accumulates, or to accept a training set where the feature is null before its launch\ndate. This is why feature sources should be append-only events wherever possible.\n\n### The online store\nA low-latency key-value store (Redis, DynamoDB, Bigtable and similar) holding only the latest value per\nentity per feature view. Serving reads it with a single lookup per entity. It is populated by\n**materialization**: a scheduled job copies the latest values from the offline store into the online\nstore, so both paths see the same computed numbers. For features that must be fresher than the\nmaterialization cadence (counts in the last minute), a streaming path writes to both stores from the\nevent stream.\n\n### Why one definition serves both paths\nThe transformation is written once and executed in the batch engine for history and in the materializer\nor stream processor for serving. Skew is prevented structurally rather than by code review, *because*\nthere is no second implementation to drift. Where a transformation depends on the request payload, two\noptions exist: run the same transformation code in both paths at request time and at training time\n(Feast's on-demand feature views work this way), or log the values actually served so they can be reused\nas training features later, closing the loop. Logging is the safer of the two when the request-time\ninputs cannot be reconstructed from history.\n\n### Versioning and lineage\nFeature view definitions are versioned; a model's training run records which versions and which offline\nsnapshot it used, which plugs directly into provenance. Changing a feature's semantics means a new\nversion, and both versions can be served during a model migration. Feature statistics (null rates,\ndistributions) computed at materialization are what drift monitoring compares against.\n\n### Where it sits in a hybrid AI system\nTypical hybrid shapes: a classical ranking or routing model decides which documents or which LLM an\nagent should use; a fraud or risk score gates whether an LLM-generated action executes; a churn model\ndecides which users get an LLM-drafted outreach. The features for those models come from a feature store;\nthe LLM gets the score or the decision, not the features. Embeddings also appear as features in these\nmodels (a user or item embedding fed to a ranker); the embedding model's version is then part of the\nfeature view's definition, so changing it is a new feature version and a retrain, for the same reason it\nforces a re-index in retrieval. If your system has none of these models, the\nanalogous concerns (consistent metadata between ingestion and query time, versioned transformations)\nare handled by the ingestion contract and embedding versioning, not by a feature store.\n\n### Products\nFeast is the common open-source option (definitions in Python, pluggable offline and online stores).\nManaged or commercial options include Tecton, the Databricks feature store, Amazon SageMaker Feature\nStore, Vertex AI Feature Store and Hopsworks; this market consolidates and renames products often, so\ncheck current offerings. The differences are mostly in streaming support, transformation execution and\nhow tightly they integrate with a given platform; the concepts above are shared.","example":"A point-in-time join in pure Python, contrasted with the leaking \"join to the latest value\" that\nhand-rolled pipelines usually do. Feature rows are `(entity, event_time, value)`; labels are\n`(entity, label_time)`.\n\n```python\nfrom bisect import bisect_right\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\n# avg_order_value_90d for customer c1, appended as it changes (offline store rows)\nfeatures = [(\"c1\", \"2024-03-01\", 40.0), (\"c1\", \"2024-04-15\", 55.0), (\"c1\", \"2024-05-20\", 90.0)]\nlabels   = [(\"c1\", \"2024-03-10\"), (\"c1\", \"2024-04-20\"), (\"c1\", \"2024-06-01\")]\nTTL_DAYS = 120\n\nby_entity = defaultdict(list)\nfor ent, ts, val in sorted(features, key=lambda r: r[1]):\n    by_entity[ent].append((ts, val))\n\ndef days_between(a, b):                     # ISO dates, good enough for the demo\n    from datetime import date\n    return (date.fromisoformat(b) - date.fromisoformat(a)).days\n\ndef point_in_time(ent, label_ts):\n    rows = by_entity[ent]\n    i = bisect_right([ts for ts, _ in rows], label_ts) - 1      # latest event_time <= label_ts\n    if i < 0 or days_between(rows[i][0], label_ts) > TTL_DAYS:\n        return None                                              # unknown at that time\n    return rows[i][1]\n\ndef leaky_latest(ent, _label_ts):\n    return by_entity[ent][-1][1]                                 # what a naive join does\n\nfor ent, lts in labels:\n    print(lts, \"correct:\", point_in_time(ent, lts), \"| leaky:\", leaky_latest(ent, lts))\n# 2024-03-10 correct: 40.0 | leaky: 90.0\n# 2024-04-20 correct: 55.0 | leaky: 90.0\n# 2024-06-01 correct: 90.0 | leaky: 90.0\n```\n\nThe March label trained with the leaky join would see a value that only became true in May. A model fit\non such rows learns relationships that cannot exist at inference time, which is exactly why offline\nmetrics overstate production performance.","tradeoffs":["A feature store removes skew and leakage by construction, but it is another platform to run and a new workflow for data scientists; below a handful of models, a shared transformation library plus a careful as-of join in SQL is a reasonable interim.","Materializing to an online store gives millisecond lookups but bounds freshness by the materialization cadence; features that must reflect the last few seconds need the streaming path, which costs more to operate.","Append-only history makes point-in-time joins possible but grows storage without bound; partition by time and expire beyond the longest TTL any model needs.","Managed stores integrate tightly with their platform and reduce operational work, but lock the feature layer to that platform; Feast keeps portability at the cost of assembling the pieces yourself.","Logging served features for later training closes the loop on online-only transformations, but adds a write per prediction to a path that was read-only; make it asynchronous and sample when traffic is high."],"pitfalls":["Joining labels to current feature values; the symptom is offline evaluation far better than production, with no bug anywhere in the model code.","Two implementations of the same feature for batch and serving; the symptom is a production accuracy drop that disappears when the serving values are logged and compared against the batch ones.","Setting no TTL on a feature view; the symptom is a model happily consuming a value computed a year ago for a dormant entity as if it were current.","Mixing event time and ingestion time; the symptom is subtle leakage when late-arriving events get stamped with when they were loaded rather than when they happened.","Adopting a feature store for an LLM-only application; the symptom is an expensive platform holding document metadata that the ingestion pipeline already handled.","Not recording feature view versions in the model's provenance; the symptom is being unable to tell why model v12 and v13 disagree after a feature definition changed.","An inclusive as-of join on a feature that counts the label event itself; the symptom is a feature that is suspiciously predictive offline and whose logged serving values sit consistently one below the training values."],"when":"Reach for a feature store when the system trains and serves more than a couple of classical models that\nshare features, when any feature is computed from time-varying history, or when training/serving skew\nhas already been observed. Do not reach for it when the only models are LLMs consuming retrieved text;\nthere the equivalent discipline lives in ingestion contracts and embedding versioning. Measure: the gap\nbetween offline and online metrics (skew or leakage shows up here first), feature freshness at serve\ntime, and the fraction of features with a single definition. In interviews for hybrid roles, expect to\nexplain the point-in-time join and why the online and offline stores must be fed from one definition;\nfor LLM-application roles, expect at most to say why you do not need one.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes feature views as code, implements the as-of join or uses the store's historical retrieval, and sets materialization schedules and TTLs per feature.","architect":"Decides whether the system is hybrid enough to justify a feature store, picks managed versus open source, and owns the freshness and consistency guarantees between offline and online paths.","fde":"Recognizes when a customer's \"AI system\" includes classical models with hand-rolled feature pipelines, and diagnoses skew and leakage before attributing problems to the LLM."},"check":{"q":"A customer's fraud model shows 0.95 AUC offline and behaves like 0.80 in production. They blame the\nmodel and want to add an LLM to \"explain\" transactions. What do you check first, and why?","a":"Check for time leakage and training/serving skew before touching the model. First, inspect how the\ntraining set was built: if labels were joined to current feature values rather than values as of each\ntransaction's timestamp, the offline score is inflated by information from the future; rebuild with a\npoint-in-time join and re-evaluate. Second, log the feature values the serving path actually sends and\ncompare them with the batch-computed values for the same entities and timestamps; any systematic\ndifference (refund handling, timezone boundaries, null defaults) is skew, and the model is being scored on\ninputs it never saw in training. Both are fixed by a single feature definition feeding an offline store\nwith history and an online store by materialization, which is what a feature store provides. An LLM\nexplanation layer would add cost and would explain decisions made on wrong inputs; it belongs, if at all,\nafter the inputs are right."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does a point-in-time join guarantee when building a training set?","options":["Each label is matched to the most recently computed feature value","Each label sees only feature values whose event time is at or before the label's timestamp","Features are computed in the same timezone as the labels","Every label has a non-null value for every feature"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The join reconstructs what was knowable when each label event occurred. Matching to the latest value leaks the future into training, and nulls are the correct outcome when no value existed yet or the TTL had expired."},{"q":"Why does a feature store keep separate offline and online stores rather than one database?","options":["Offline stores are cheaper, so all data should live there","Online stores can run SQL joins faster","Training needs full history for as-of joins while serving needs millisecond lookups of only the latest values","Regulations require training data to be isolated from production"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The two access patterns conflict: history and heavy scans versus single-key latency. Materialization copies the latest values from the historical store to the key-value store so both paths see the same computed numbers."},{"q":"A team building a pure RAG assistant asks whether they need a feature store for document metadata. What is the right guidance?","options":["Yes, because metadata filters are features of the retrieval model","Yes, because the online store will speed up metadata lookups","No; without classical models to train and serve, the ingestion contract and embedding versioning cover their needs","Only if they use more than one embedding model"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Feature stores solve training/serving skew and time leakage for models trained on tabular features. A RAG assistant has no such model; consistency between ingestion and query metadata is an ingestion contract concern, and transformation versioning is handled by the embedding recipe."}]}]});
